// Sections 116-127: memory and context.

render_section_116 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Every procedure body sees one implicit value named `context`. It takes
        \\no import, no parameter and no declaration: the compiler creates the
        \\type, creates one instance of it, and makes the name visible
        \\everywhere. Allocation, logging and random-number
        \\generation all read their policy out of that one value, which is how a
        \\caller redirects a library's behaviour without changing a single
        \\signature the library declares.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The type is built into the compiler rather than written in a `.glo`
        \\file, and its spelling carries the directive sigil to say so: it is
        \\`#Context`. The definition is fixed and has six fields.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\Context :: struct {
        \\    allocator:        Allocator;
        \\    temp_allocator:   Allocator;
        \\    logger:           Logger;
        \\    random_generator: Random_Generator;
        \\    user_ptr:         ?*void;
        \\    user_index:       ssize;
        \\}
    );
    table_header(3, "Field   —   What reads it");
    table_row(5, "`allocator`   |   `alloc`, `free`, `array_add`, `format`, and every standard-library call that defaults its allocator parameter");
    table_row(6, "`temp_allocator`   |   `tprint`, and everything else that returns arena-backed storage");
    table_row(7, "`logger`   |   structured logging; the vtable is zeroed in the default context");
    table_row(8, "`random_generator`   |   the random-number vtable; also zeroed in the default context");
    table_row(9, "`user_ptr`   |   nothing; reserved for the program");
    table_row(10, "`user_index`   |   nothing; reserved for the program");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`user_ptr` and `user_index` exist so that a program can thread its own
        \\state through code it does not own, in the same way the allocator is
        \\threaded. Nothing in the compiler or the standard library reads them.
    );
    subheading(12, "Reading and writing `context`");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\`context` is an ordinary lvalue of type `#Context`. Its fields can be
        \\read, assigned, copied into a local and passed to a procedure that
        \\declares a `#Context` parameter. A bare assignment to a field is
        \\permanent: the context is one process-wide global, not a per-call
        \\record, so nothing restores it later.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\with_index :: (base: #Context, index: ssize) -> #Context {
        \\    out := base;
        \\    out.user_index = index;
        \\    return out;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    context.user_index = 7;
        \\    later := with_index(context, 9);
        \\}
    );
    subheading(15, "`#push_context`");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`#push_context expr body` installs `expr` as the whole context for the
        \\duration of `body` and restores the previous value on the way out,
        \\including through an early `return`. The restore is scheduled as a
        \\`defer`, so it is not skipped by any exit the block has.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    context.user_index = 7;
        \\    scoped := context;
        \\    scoped.user_index = 42;
        \\    #push_context scoped {
        \\        print("scoped %\n", context.user_index);
        \\    }
        \\    print("restored %\n", context.user_index);
        \\    print("hexadecimal %x\n", 255);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\scoped 42
        \\restored 7
        \\hexadecimal ff
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Written without an expression, `#push_context body` installs the
        \\default context, the same value the process started with. That is the
        \\way to run a piece of code under known settings regardless of what an
        \\enclosing frame installed.
        \\Numeric formatting is selected explicitly with `Formatter` selectors
        \\such as `%x`; the former `context.print_style` field no longer exists.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    context.user_index = 7;
        \\    print("outer %\n", context.user_index);
        \\    #push_context {
        \\        print("bare %\n", context.user_index);
        \\    }
        \\    print("outer again %\n", context.user_index);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\outer 7
        \\bare 0
        \\outer again 7
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The body may be a brace block or a single statement, so
        \\`#push_context saved print("%\n", context.user_index);` is legal and
        \\scopes to that one statement.
    );
    subheading(23, "`#push_allocator`");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`#push_allocator(expr);` replaces only `context.allocator`. It is a
        \\statement rather than a block form: it takes effect where it is
        \\written and is undone at the end of the enclosing block, again through
        \\a deferred restore that also runs on `return`.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\served :: () -> bool {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    if #pattern .Some(raw) = p {
        \\        free(p);
        \\        return true;
        \\    }
        \\    return false;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    {
        \\        #push_allocator(nil_allocator());
        \\        print("nil served %\n", served());
        \\    }
        \\    print("default served %\n", served());
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\nil served 0
        \\default served 1
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Both directives are statements. At file scope `#push_allocator(...)`
        \\is `error: bad decl name 'push_allocator'`; a whole-program default is
        \\set by assigning `context.allocator` at the top of `main` instead.
    );
    note(28, #string,oneline
        \\`#push_allocator` changes where memory comes from, not who owns it.
        \\An `alloc` inside `#push_allocator(context.temp_allocator)` still
        \\carries a release obligation and still warns
        \\`[memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released
        \\by 'free' on every path`. Ownership is the subject of
        \\Ownership and Release Obligations.
    );
    subheading(29, "`#no_context`");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\A procedure marked `#no_context` may not name `context` in its body.
        \\`#c_call` implies the same restriction, because a procedure entered
        \\from foreign code cannot assume the context global was ever
        \\initialised by Glosso startup.
    );
    code_block(31, "glosso", #string
        \\hash :: (n: s64) -> s64 #no_context {
        \\    return n * 31 + context.user_index;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(32, "text", #string
        \\error: cannot access 'context' in a #c_call / #no_context procedure
        \\  --> ...\bare_fn.glo:2:21
        \\  |
        \\2 |     return n * 31 + context.user_index;
        \\  |                     ^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\The restriction is on the name, not on the machinery. A `#no_context`
        \\procedure may still call `print` or `alloc`, and those callees read the
        \\context as usual, because the context is a global rather than a hidden
        \\parameter. `#no_context` is also part of a procedure's overload key,
        \\and a `#fn_ptr` type accepts the directive after its return type.
    );
    subheading(34, "Naming a context field type");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\`#Context` and its fields are always reachable. The types of those
        \\fields are not: they are declared in the `Context` module and stay
        \\hidden until a file imports it, so that a program which never spells
        \\one out does not have the supporting type names dropped into its global
        \\namespace. Naming one without the import reports both the cause and
        \\the fix.
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\error: 'Allocator' is a #Context field type; add `#import "Context";` to name it
        \\  --> ...\field_type.glo:1:13
        \\  |
        \\1 | pool :: (a: Allocator) -> s64 {
        \\  |             ^^^^^^^^^
    );
    table_header(37, "Context field   —   Types it introduces");
    table_row(39, "`allocator`, `temp_allocator`   |   `Allocator`, `Allocator_Proc`, `Allocator_Mode`, `Allocator_Error`");
    table_row(40, "`logger`   |   `Logger`, `Logger_Proc`, `Logger_Level`");
    table_row(41, "`random_generator`   |   `Random_Generator`, `Random_Generator_Proc`, `Random_Generator_Mode`");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\The gate opens transitively: `#import "Io"` reaches `Context` through
        \\`Format`, so a file that prints can also name `Allocator`. Reading and
        \\writing `context.allocator` never needs the import; only spelling one
        \\of these names as a type does. `Context` written without the sigil is
        \\`error: the built-in Context type is written #Context`.
        \\This special type-name gate does not re-export the module's procedures:
        \\import `Context` directly to call `alloc`, `free`, or allocator helpers.
    );
    note(43, #string,oneline
        \\The compile-time VM has its own readable and writable `context`.
        \\Changing it does not initialize the native program's runtime context.
        \\This does not make every context operation available in the VM:
        \\a direct `alloc(16)` still stops with `#magic 'ctx_alloc' has no VM
        \\implementation`. Check the operation, rather than assuming that
        \\reading an allocator record makes its native allocation path usable.
        \\Compile-time evaluation is described in `#comptime`.
    );
    code_block(44, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#comptime {
        \\    context.user_index = 7;
        \\    print("compile-time context %\n", context.user_index);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("runtime context %\n", context.user_index);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(45, "text", #string
        \\compile-time context 7
        \\runtime context 0
    );
}

render_section_117 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso ships no allocation policy in the language. An allocator is a
        \\value: a procedure pointer and an opaque data pointer, declared in the
        \\`Context` module and stored in `context.allocator`. Every allocation
        \\the language performs on a program's behalf goes through that one
        \\record, which is what makes an arena, a pool or a tracking wrapper a
        \\drop-in replacement rather than a rewrite.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Allocator_Proc :: #fn_ptr(
        \\    data: ?*void,
        \\    mode: Allocator_Mode,
        \\    size: ssize,
        \\    alignment: ssize,
        \\    old_memory: ?*void,
        \\    old_size: ssize
        \\) -> ?*void
        \\    #memory{unknown(result: returned, reason: "allocator mode determines result provenance"),
        \\            reads(data, old_memory), writes(data, old_memory),
        \\            noescape(data, old_memory), maybe_kills(old_memory)};
        \\
        \\Allocator :: struct { procedure: ?Allocator_Proc; data: ?*void; }
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\Four of the slots are optional, and each for a reason. A stateless
        \\allocator carries no `data`; `Alloc` and `Free_All` pass no
        \\`old_memory`; an unsatisfiable request yields no memory; and
        \\`procedure` itself is optional so that a zero-initialised `Allocator`
        \\is the "none installed" sentinel. Because `.None` is address zero,
        \\that sentinel is still the all-zero bit pattern.
    );
    subheading(3, "Modes");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\One procedure serves every operation; `mode` selects which. The enum
        \\is closed.
    );
    table_header(5, "Mode   —   Request   —   Value");
    table_row(6, "`Alloc`   |   return `size` bytes at `alignment`   |   0");
    table_row(7, "`Free`   |   release `old_memory`   |   1");
    table_row(8, "`Free_All`   |   release everything this allocator owns   |   2");
    table_row(9, "`Resize`   |   move `old_memory` from `old_size` to `size`   |   3");
    table_row(10, "`Query_Features`   |   report which modes are implemented   |   4");
    table_row(11, "`Query_Info`   |   report allocator metadata   |   5");
    table_row(12, "`Alloc_Non_Zeroed`   |   `Alloc` without the obligation to zero   |   6");
    table_row(13, "`Resize_Non_Zeroed`   |   `Resize` without zeroing the new tail   |   7");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\An allocator may implement a subset and return `.None` for the rest;
        \\that is what the supplied ones do. `Resize` is not optional in
        \\practice, because a dynamic array grown by `array_add` and a
        \\`String_Builder` grown by `write_string` both issue it, and an
        \\allocator that answers `.None` there hands the caller no memory to
        \\write into.
    );
    subheading(15, "Errors");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`Allocator_Error` names the ways a request can fail. Its variants
        \\carry explicit values so that the enum is stable across versions, and
        \\`allocator_error_reason` turns one into a message.
    );
    table_header(17, "Variant   —   Reason string   —   Value");
    table_row(18, "`None`   |   `no error`   |   0");
    table_row(19, "`Out_Of_Memory`   |   `out of memory`   |   1");
    table_row(20, "`Invalid_Pointer`   |   `invalid pointer`   |   2");
    table_row(21, "`Invalid_Argument`   |   `invalid argument`   |   3");
    table_row(22, "`Mode_Not_Implemented`   |   `allocator mode is not implemented`   |   4");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`Allocator_Error` carries an `Error` instance, so a program that
        \\propagates one gets the reporting described in `Error`,
        \\`Termination`, and `main`. No entry point in the allocation API
        \\returns one today: an `Allocator_Proc` signals failure by returning
        \\`.None`, and so do `alloc` and `mem_alloc`.
    );
    subheading(24, "The supplied allocators");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The `Context` module supplies four allocators, each behind a
        \\constructor that builds the two-field record. All four are stateless
        \\and leave `data` empty.
    );
    table_header(26, "Constructor   —   Modes it answers   —   Backs onto");
    table_row(27, "`heap_allocator()`   |   `Alloc`, `Alloc_Non_Zeroed`, `Resize`, `Resize_Non_Zeroed`, `Free`   |   the process heap");
    table_row(28, "`debug_allocator()`   |   the same five, plus `Free_All`, which prints the leak report   |   the heap, with bookkeeping");
    table_row(29, "`default_temp_allocator()`, `temp_allocator()`   |   `Alloc`, `Alloc_Non_Zeroed`, `Free_All`   |   the temporary arena");
    table_row(30, "`nil_allocator()`   |   none; every request answers `.None`   |   nothing");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\The default context installs the debug allocator in
        \\`context.allocator` when `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` is set to any value
        \\other than `0`; otherwise it installs the heap allocator at every
        \\optimization level. It always installs the default temporary allocator
        \\in `context.temp_allocator`. The environment-controlled swap is the
        \\subject of The Debug Allocator.
    );
    subheading(32, "Allocating");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\Two layers sit on top of the vtable. `alloc` and `free` route through
        \\`context.allocator` and are the everyday pair; the `mem_*` family
        \\takes an explicit allocator, defaulting to `context.allocator` when
        \\the argument is omitted.
    );
    table_header(34, "Call   —   What it does");
    table_row(35, "`alloc`   |   `alloc(n) -> ?*void`; `Alloc` through `context.allocator` at alignment 16");
    table_row(36, "`free`   |   `free(p)`; `Free` through `context.allocator`");
    table_row(37, "`mem_alloc`   |   `mem_alloc(size, allocator := context.allocator)`; `Alloc` at alignment 16");
    table_row(38, "`mem_resize`   |   `mem_resize(old, old_size, size, allocator := context.allocator)`; the old pointer is invalidated");
    table_row(39, "`mem_free`   |   `mem_free(p, allocator := context.allocator)`");
    table_row(40, "`free_all`   |   `free_all(allocator := context.allocator)`; sends `Free_All`");
    table_row(41, "`allocator_mem_alloc`, `allocator_mem_free`   |   the same allocate and release operations with the allocator written first");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\`mem_free` accepts a `?*void`, so an allocation result is handed
        \\straight back to it; `mem_resize` requires a plain `*void`, so the
        \\result has to be matched first. An `Allocator` whose `procedure` slot
        \\is empty serves nothing and releases nothing rather than calling
        \\through it.
    );
    code_block(43, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := mem_alloc(8, heap_allocator());
        \\    if #pattern .Some(raw) = p {
        \\        cell := cast(*s64) raw;
        \\        cell.* = 42;
        \\        print("cell %\n", cell.*);
        \\    }
        \\    mem_free(p, heap_allocator());
        \\
        \\    q := mem_alloc(8, nil_allocator());
        \\    served := false;
        \\    if #pattern .Some(raw) = q served = true;
        \\    print("nil served %\n", served);
        \\    mem_free(q, nil_allocator());
        \\}
    );
    code_block(44, "text", #string
        \\cell 42
        \\nil served 0
    );
    subheading(45, "Writing an allocator");
    paragraph(46, #string,oneline
        \\An allocator is one procedure with the `Allocator_Proc` shape plus a
        \\constructor that packages it. The procedure switches on `mode` and
        \\returns `.None` for anything it does not handle. Installing it with
        \\`#push_allocator` redirects every allocation in the block, including
        \\the ones made inside library code such as `format`.
    );
    code_block(47, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\#import "String_Builder";
        \\
        \\requests: ssize = 0;
        \\
        \\tracking_proc :: (
        \\    data: ?*void,
        \\    mode: Allocator_Mode,
        \\    size: ssize,
        \\    alignment: ssize,
        \\    old_memory: ?*void,
        \\    old_size: ssize
        \\) -> ?*void #memory{unknown, noescape(data, old_memory),
        \\        maybe_kills(old_memory), maybe_invalidates(old_memory)} {
        \\    heap := heap_allocator();
        \\    if mode == {
        \\        case .Alloc, .Alloc_Non_Zeroed;
        \\            requests += 1;
        \\            return mem_alloc(size, heap);
        \\        case .Resize, .Resize_Non_Zeroed;
        \\            requests += 1;
        \\            if #pattern .Some(old) = old_memory {
        \\                return mem_resize(old, old_size, size, heap);
        \\            }
        \\            return mem_alloc(size, heap);
        \\        case .Free;
        \\            mem_free(old_memory, heap);
        \\            return .None;
        \\        else;
        \\            return .None;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\tracking_allocator :: () -> Allocator #memory returns_static {
        \\    a: Allocator;
        \\    a.procedure = tracking_proc;
        \\    a.data = .None;
        \\    return a;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #push_allocator(tracking_allocator());
        \\    text := format("%-%", 1, 2);
        \\    print("% after % request(s)\n", text, requests);
        \\    string_free(text);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(48, "text", #string
        \\1-2 after 1 request(s)
    );
    paragraph(49, #string,oneline
        \\The `#memory` contract on the procedure is what keeps the memory
        \\analysis quiet about a body that hands out storage it did not create:
        \\`unknown` states that the result's lifetime is not described by the
        \\contract system, `noescape(data, old_memory)` states that neither
        \\pointer argument is retained, and the two `maybe_` clauses declare the
        \\release and the invalidation the `Free` and `Resize` arms perform on
        \\some paths. Without them the body reports `[memory.I5] body of
        \\'tracking_proc' has undeclared kills effect on 'old_memory'`. Writing
        \\`#memory` Contracts covers the
        \\vocabulary.
    );
    note(50, #string,oneline
        \\The standard library's convention for a routine that allocates is a
        \\trailing `allocator: Allocator = context.allocator` parameter, as in
        \\`clone`, `builder_make_len_cap` and the `Container` constructors. A
        \\caller that wants a different allocator for one call passes it; a
        \\caller that wants it for a region uses `#push_allocator`.
    );
}

render_section_118 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`context.temp_allocator` is a growing bump arena. It hands out
        \\successive slices of one block, ignores `Free` entirely, and reclaims
        \\everything at once when the arena is rewound. It exists for values
        \\that are built, used and abandoned inside a bounded region: a
        \\formatted line, a path joined for one system call, a line of input.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The initial block is not heap memory. It is a static buffer compiled
        \\into the binary, sized by `--temp-allocator-size`, which defaults to
        \\4 MiB, or to 64 KiB on a freestanding target. When a request does not
        \\fit, the arena mallocs an overflow
        \\block whose capacity is the larger of the request and the initial
        \\size, and links it onto the front; rewinding pops and releases those
        \\blocks back down to the static one.
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --backend llvm-ir -o report.ll report.glo
        \\$ grep "^@glo_temp_buf" report.ll
        \\@glo_temp_buf = internal global [4194304 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 16
        \\
        \\$ glosso --backend llvm-ir --temp-allocator-size 65536 -o report.ll report.glo
        \\$ grep "^@glo_temp_buf" report.ll
        \\@glo_temp_buf = internal global [65536 x i8] zeroinitializer, align 16
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\`--temp-allocator-size <bytes>` and `--temp-allocator-size=<bytes>`
        \\both work. The value must be a positive byte count; anything else,
        \\including `0`, is rejected before compilation starts and the driver
        \\exits with status 2.
    );
    code_block(4, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --temp-allocator-size 0 report.glo
        \\glosso: --temp-allocator-size must be a positive byte count
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Whether each thread gets its own arena is a target decision. The
        \\buffer and the three globals that track it are emitted `thread_local`
        \\on some targets and shared by every thread on others, Windows among
        \\them. The context itself is never thread-local anywhere, so a thread
        \\that wants its own settings installs them with `#push_context`.
    );
    subheading(6, "Frame boundaries");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The arena is rewound by the compiler, not by the program. Lowering
        \\records the arena's high-water mark on entry to a body and restores it
        \\on the way out, so an ordinary block is already a temporary frame.
        \\Nothing has to be freed and nothing accumulates.
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\The marks are emitted only where temporary storage provably cannot
        \\leave with the result. A signature is treated as able to carry it out,
        \\and therefore gets no marks anywhere in its body, when either of the
        \\following holds.
    );
    bullet(9, #string,oneline
        \\The return type is a `string`, an `any`, a struct, a union, a pointer,
        \\a many-pointer, an array or a tuple.
    );
    bullet(10, #string,oneline
        \\Any parameter is a typed pointer, `*T` or `[*]T`.
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\A loop body in a procedure that satisfies neither reuses the same
        \\arena bytes on every iteration.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    first: s64 = 0;
        \\    for i: 0..3 {
        \\        row := tprint("row-%", i);
        \\        here := cast(s64) row.data;
        \\        if i == 0 first = here;
        \\        print("% reuses the first block: %\n", row, here == first);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\row-0 reuses the first block: 1
        \\row-1 reuses the first block: 1
        \\row-2 reuses the first block: 1
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The contrast is visible in one program. `scalar` returns an `s64`, so
        \\its mark is restored and two consecutive calls hand back the same
        \\address. `carrier` takes a `*s64`, so no mark is taken and its two
        \\temporaries sit sixteen bytes apart.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\scalar :: () -> s64 {
        \\    return cast(s64) tprint("aaaa").data;
        \\}
        \\
        \\carrier :: (sink: *s64) {
        \\    first := cast(s64) tprint("aaaa").data;
        \\    second := cast(s64) tprint("bbbb").data;
        \\    sink.* = second - first;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("scalar reuse %\n", scalar() == scalar());
        \\    delta: s64 = 0;
        \\    carrier(*delta);
        \\    print("carrier delta %\n", delta);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\scalar reuse 1
        \\carrier delta 16
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\This is exactly why `tprint` can return arena memory at all. The
        \\return type is `string`, so no restore is emitted in `tprint` or in
        \\any caller whose own signature also carries storage out, and the
        \\result survives until the nearest enclosing frame that does take a
        \\mark closes.
    );
    note(18, #string,oneline
        \\A temporary value assigned out of an inner block is stale the moment
        \\the block ends. The compiler does not diagnose it; the bytes are
        \\handed to the next request that asks for them.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    held: string;
        \\    {
        \\        held = tprint("inner-%", 1);
        \\    }
        \\    _ := tprint("ABCDEFG");
        \\    print("held=%\n", held);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\held=ABCDEFG
    );
    subheading(21, "`reset_temporary_storage`");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`reset_temporary_storage()` calls `free_all(context.temp_allocator)`,
        \\which sends `Free_All` to the arena: overflow blocks are released and
        \\the mark returns to the start of the static block. It is the explicit
        \\spelling of what the compiler already does at a frame boundary.
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Every pointer, string, slice, and returned aggregate still referring to
        \\the reset arena becomes invalid. The memory checker follows recognized
        \\temporary allocator origins through scoped `#push_allocator`, Context
        \\allocation calls, dynamic-array growth, and returned values. A later use
        \\is diagnosed as stale when `reset_temporary_storage` or `free_all` resets
        \\that arena. Allocations from the ordinary heap remain live, and new
        \\allocations after the reset belong to a new storage generation.
        \\Arbitrary allocator procedures and direct replacement of context fields
        \\are outside this analysis; keep temporary values inside their lifetime
        \\even when no diagnostic is emitted.
    );
    subheading(24, "What comes from temporary storage");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A value backed by the arena carries no release obligation and must not
        \\be passed to `free` or `string_free`. These are the standard-library
        \\entry points that produce one.
    );
    table_header(26, "Call   —   Result");
    table_row(27, "`tprint`   |   the formatted string, in the arena");
    table_row(28, "`read_line`   |   the `text` field of its `Read_Line`, in the arena");
    table_row(29, "`c_string`   |   a NUL-terminated copy of the string, in the arena");
    table_row(30, "`format`   |   the formatted string, when it is passed `.allocator = context.temp_allocator` and transferred with `string_into_temporary`");
    table_row(31, "`builder_into_temporary_string`   |   the builder's arena-backed bytes as a string");
    table_row(32, "`string_into_temporary`   |   the same string with its release obligation dropped");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`print` and `writef` build their output with `tprint`, and `write_int`
        \\formats through the arena as well, so a procedure that prints is
        \\already using temporary storage even if it never names it. `format` is
        \\the owning counterpart: it allocates from `context.allocator` and its
        \\result is released with `string_free`.
    );
    note(34, #string,oneline
        \\A temporary value that has to outlive its frame is copied, not
        \\retained. `clone(text)` and `format("%", value)` both produce an owned
        \\string from `context.allocator`; writing `kept = clone(tprint(...))`
        \\inside the inner block above makes the earlier example print
        \\`kept=inner-1`. Built-in Strings covers the string representation
        \\itself.
    );
}

render_section_119 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Set `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` to any value other than `0` when compiling
        \\to make the default `context.allocator` the debug allocator instead of
        \\the heap allocator. With the variable absent or equal to `0`, the heap
        \\allocator remains the default. This choice is independent of `-O`,
        \\`--borrow-check`, and the sanitizers.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Every allocation it serves gets a 48-byte header holding the size, the
        \\source trace of the request and links into a live list, plus an
        \\eight-byte guard written past the end of the user bytes. That
        \\bookkeeping is what lets the allocator name the line a leaked block
        \\came from, and it is why diagnostic mode must be requested explicitly.
    );
    note(41, #string,oneline
        \\A program can still install `debug_allocator()` explicitly. That routes
        \\allocations and releases through the wrapper, but the compiler-wide
        \\`GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` mode additionally instruments pointer uses and
        \\requests the automatic report on normal exit, `exit`, failed `#assert`,
        \\and `process_abort`.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    kept := alloc(64) #memory leak;
        \\    scratch := alloc(32);
        \\    free(scratch);
        \\    print("done\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --run trace.glo
        \\done
        \\glosso debug allocator: leaked 1 allocation(s), 64 byte(s)
        \\  leak: 64 byte(s)
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      ...\trace.glo:5:13
        \\
        \\$ glosso -O0 --run trace.glo
        \\done
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The report gives one `leak:` entry per block still live at exit, with
        \\the byte count and the trace of the call that produced it. A dynamic
        \\array is reported at its `array_add`, because that is the call that
        \\asked the allocator for storage.
    );
    subheading(5, "Double free");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\A second `free` of a block the allocator already reclaimed is reported
        \\with all three positions: where the block came from, where it was
        \\first released, and where the second release happened.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    free(p);
        \\    free(p);
        \\    print("still running\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --borrow-check off --run twice.glo
        \\glosso debug allocator: double free
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      ...\twice.glo:5:10
        \\    first free at:
        \\      ...\twice.glo:6:5
        \\    second free at:
        \\      ...\twice.glo:7:5
        \\still running
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check off` is needed only to get past the compile-time
        \\diagnostic, which catches this particular program on its own with
        \\`warning: [memory.double_release] double release of resource`. The
        \\two mechanisms answer
        \\different questions: the analysis reasons about the source, the debug
        \\allocator reports what the process actually did. Where the analysis
        \\cannot see, and Limits of the Memory Analysis lists where that is, the
        \\allocator still can.
    );
    subheading(10, "Use after free");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\When debug-allocator mode is enabled, the backend inserts a check before
        \\every load and store through a pointer at every optimization level. If
        \\the address falls inside a block on the freed list, the read or write is
        \\reported and then allowed to proceed.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    if #pattern .Some(raw) = p {
        \\        bytes := cast([*]u8) raw;
        \\        bytes[0] = 7;
        \\        free(p);
        \\        print("value=%\n", bytes[0]);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --borrow-check off --run after.glo
        \\glosso debug allocator: use after free
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      ...\after.glo:4:10
        \\    freed at:
        \\      ...\after.glo:8:9
        \\    used at:
        \\      ...\after.glo:9:28
        \\value=7
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    subheading(14, "Invalid free and invalid realloc");
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\A pointer the debug allocator never handed out, and that is not on its
        \\freed list either, is an invalid free. Only the release site can be
        \\named, because there is no header to read a history from.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    slot: s64 = 1;
        \\    free(cast(*void) *slot);
        \\    print("continued\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --borrow-check off --run notmine.glo
        \\glosso debug allocator: invalid free
        \\    free at:
        \\      ...\notmine.glo:6:5
        \\continued
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\The same test on the resize path is `invalid realloc`. Its output also
        \\shows what a trace is: not one position but the chain of call sites
        \\that reached the allocator, innermost first. The single-line traces
        \\above are single-line only because `alloc` and `free` lower directly
        \\at the point of use.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    slot: s64 = 1;
        \\    fresh := mem_resize(cast(*void) *slot, 8, 32);
        \\    print("continued\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --borrow-check off --run badresize.glo
        \\glosso debug allocator: invalid realloc
        \\    realloc at:
        \\      ...\std\Context.glo:309:50
        \\      ...\std\Context.glo:502:16
        \\      ...\badresize.glo:6:14
        \\continued
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    subheading(21, "The tail guard");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Eight bytes of `0xa5` are written past the end of every allocation and
        \\checked on `Free` and again when the block is walked for the leak
        \\report. A write that runs off the end of the requested size damages
        \\the guard and is counted.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(16);
        \\    if #pattern .Some(raw) = p {
        \\        bytes := cast([*]u8) raw;
        \\        bytes[16] = 1;
        \\    }
        \\    free(p);
        \\    print("continued\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --run overrun.glo
        \\continued
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A damaged guard has no message of its own; it raises the error count
        \\and nothing else. An error total with no fault report above it is
        \\therefore an overrun, and the block that carried it is found by
        \\narrowing the writes rather than by reading the output.
    );
    subheading(26, "When the report runs");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\The report is emitted at most once per process, guarded by a flag the
        \\report itself sets. It runs on a normal return from `main`, and also
        \\before `exit`, before a failed `#assert` aborts and before an explicit
        \\process abort, so a program that stops deliberately still says what it
        \\was holding. It does not run on the safety-check path: a bounds or
        \\overflow failure at `-O0` prints its runtime-check category and then
        \\terminates without running the allocator report, as Runtime Safety
        \\Checks and Panics describes.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`debug_allocator_report_leaks()` forces it early. Because of the
        \\once-only flag, forcing it means nothing allocated afterwards is ever
        \\reported. The magic behind it, `__debug_report_leaks`, sits in a
        \\private section of the `Context` module and naming it directly is
        \\`error: unknown function __debug_report_leaks`. The same report is
        \\what `free_all(context.allocator)` triggers while the debug allocator
        \\is installed, because `Free_All` is the mode it maps to.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    first := alloc(64) #memory leak;
        \\    debug_allocator_report_leaks();
        \\    second := alloc(32) #memory leak;
        \\    print("done\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 --run early.glo
        \\glosso debug allocator: leaked 1 allocation(s), 64 byte(s)
        \\  leak: 64 byte(s)
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      ...\early.glo:5:14
        \\done
    );
    subheading(31, "The message set");
    table_header(32, "Fault   —   Message after `glosso debug allocator:`   —   Traces");
    table_row(33, "leak   |   `leaked N allocation(s), M byte(s)`, then a `leak:` entry per block   |   `allocated at:`");
    table_row(34, "double free   |   `double free`   |   `allocated at:`, `first free at:`, `second free at:`");
    table_row(35, "use after free   |   `use after free`   |   `allocated at:`, `freed at:`, `used at:`");
    table_row(36, "invalid free   |   `invalid free`   |   `free at:`");
    table_row(37, "invalid realloc   |   `invalid realloc`   |   `realloc at:`");
    table_row(38, "total   |   `N allocator error(s)`, printed last   |   none");
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\None of them terminates the process. Each fault is recorded, execution
        \\continues, and the total is printed with the report; the exit status
        \\is unaffected, so a build step that wants a failure has to read the
        \\output.
    );
    note(40, #string,oneline
        \\The debug allocator sees only what passes through it. Storage from
        \\`context.temp_allocator` is never tracked, because the arena serves
        \\itself out of a static buffer, and neither is memory taken from a
        \\foreign `malloc` or from an allocator installed with
        \\`#push_allocator`.
    );
}

render_section_120 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso has no garbage collector, no destructors, and no ownership in
        \\its type system. Every allocation is released by an explicit call, and
        \\the compiler's work is to say when that call is missing, duplicated, or
        \\made too early. Five separate machineries do it: three during
        \\compilation and two inside the emitted program.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Acting on a memory diagnostic starts with deciding which of the five
        \\produced it. They differ in severity, in what silences them, and in
        \\whether the claim is proved or merely assumed.
    );
    table_header(2, "Mechanism   —   When it runs   —   Severity");
    table_row(3, "`#memory` contract validation   |   before any analysis   |   always an error");
    table_row(4, "temporal analysis   |   after type checking, before lowering   |   set by `--borrow-check`, warning by default");
    table_row(5, "static semantics   |   during type checking   |   always an error, relaxed only by `#enable`");
    table_row(6, "runtime safety checks   |   in the program, at `-O0` and `-O1`   |   report a category, then take the target's abort path (134 on hosted Windows and Linux x86-64; 125 on WASI)");
    table_row(7, "debug allocator   |   in the program, when `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` is nonzero   |   report on stderr, the program continues");

    subheading(8, "Contract validation");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The validator reads every `#memory` directive and checks it against the
        \\declaration it annotates: that a named parameter exists, that a releaser
        \\is reachable and itself declares `kills` or `owns` on a memory-capable
        \\parameter on every path, that a return place names a real union variant
        \\or tuple element, and that the clauses do not contradict one another.
        \\None of that depends on how the program behaves, so a failure is a hard
        \\error. An unrecognised clause name never reaches the validator: it is a
        \\parse error, `error: unknown #memory effect 'reclaims'`.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\count :: (x: ssize) -> ssize #memory returns_fresh { return x; }
        \\
        \\main :: () { }
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\error: [memory.contract] #memory returns_fresh requires 'count' to return a memory-capable value
        \\  --> contract.glo:1:34
        \\  |
        \\1 | count :: (x: ssize) -> ssize #memory returns_fresh { return x; }
        \\  |                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check off` does not silence this class. The flag chooses the
        \\severity of the analysis, and an unusable contract leaves nothing to
        \\analyse. The flag has its own chapter; the clause vocabulary is in the
        \\chapter on writing `#memory` contracts.
    );

    subheading(13, "Temporal analysis");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The second mechanism is an abstract interpreter over the checked
        \\program. It walks each function body, tracking every resource through
        \\assignments, calls, branches, `defer`, and `return`, and reports the
        \\states that contradict a release obligation: leaks, double releases,
        \\use after release, release through a function other than the declared
        \\releaser, stale references, overlapping exclusive references, and
        \\pointers to local storage that escape the frame owning them.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    array_add(*values, 2);
        \\    print("count=%\n", values.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'array_free' before leaving scope
        \\  --> counts.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> counts.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\At the default setting that is a warning: the program still links, still
        \\runs, and prints `count=2`. Under `--borrow-check error` the identical
        \\text appears with `error:` in place of `warning:`, no executable is
        \\produced, and the compiler exits 1.
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory` is a compile-time contract only. It changes no layout, no
        \\calling convention, and no generated instruction; deleting every
        \\directive from a program changes which diagnostics appear and nothing
        \\else. A contract that lies therefore costs a silence, never a
        \\miscompilation.
    );

    subheading(19, "Static semantics");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\A third group of memory-relevant rules is enforced by the type checker.
        \\`*T`, `[*]T`, and `#fn_ptr` exclude zero so that `?*T`, `?[*]T`, and
        \\`?#fn_ptr` can spend the null value on their `.None` case, and a
        \\tagged-union payload may only be reached by a pattern match. The second
        \\rule is what stops the `?*void` returned by `alloc` from being read as a
        \\pointer without a test.
    );
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    print("%\n", p.Some);
        \\    free(p);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\error: tagged-union payload '.Some' may only be bound by a pattern match
        \\  --> payload.glo:6:20
        \\  |
        \\6 |     print("%\n", p.Some);
        \\  |                    ^^^^
        \\note: match on the union and use that arm's payload capture, or enable #enable(allow_union_unwrap) for unsafe access
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\These are errors, and `--borrow-check` has no bearing on them. The only
        \\relaxation is a feature gate, described in the chapter on feature gates.
    );

    subheading(24, "The two runtime layers");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\Runtime safety checks are instructions in the emitted program: array
        \\bounds, integer overflow, a `cast(*T)` of a zero address, and the gated
        \\union unwrap. They are emitted only when the optimization level is 0 or
        \\1, and every failure reports its category before taking the target's
        \\abort path. The chapter on
        \\runtime safety checks and panics lists the full family.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The debug allocator is a wrapper installed as `context.allocator` when
        \\`GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` has a value other than `0`, independently of
        \\the optimization level. It records an allocation trace for every block
        \\and reports leaks, double frees, use after free, invalid frees, and
        \\invalid reallocations on stderr. It never aborts; it prints an error
        \\count when the process ends. Its own chapter covers the reports in detail.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Neither runtime layer knows anything about the compile-time ones, and
        \\silencing one does not silence the other. `#memory leak` tells the
        \\analyser that an allocation is deliberately never released, which
        \\removes the compile-time obligation and nothing more.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    table := alloc(64) #memory leak;
        \\    print("interned for the life of the process\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\interned for the life of the process
        \\glosso debug allocator: leaked 1 allocation(s), 64 byte(s)
        \\  leak: 64 byte(s)
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      keep.glo:5:14
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\That output appears when `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR` is nonzero, at any
        \\optimization level. With the variable absent or equal to `0`, the same
        \\program prints its own line and nothing else, because the report belongs
        \\to the allocator rather than the runtime-safety-check level.
    );

    subheading(31, "What is proved and what is assumed");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Inside one non-generic function body, along the paths the interpreter
        \\walks, a temporal diagnostic is a statement about the program. Every
        \\claim that crosses a function boundary rests on a contract instead, and
        \\several constructs are not modelled at all.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\A contract on a declaration with no body, which is every `#foreign`
        \\declaration and most `#magic` ones, is an axiom. Nothing checks it
        \\against an implementation because there is no implementation to check.
    );
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\A contract on a function that does have a body is compared with the
        \\summary inferred from that body, and disagreement is reported, as in
        \\`[memory.contract] body of 'make' returns a resource released by 'free', but its #memory contract requires 'c_free'`.
    );
    bullet(35, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory unknown` asserts nothing and pessimizes nothing. It records
        \\that the analyser knows nothing about the function, which is what it
        \\already assumed of an uncontracted one.
    );
    bullet(36, #string,oneline
        \\A generic function body is analysed on the checker's skolem copy of it,
        \\where a type parameter conservatively carries memory, and once per
        \\template rather than once per instantiation. A template whose body could
        \\not be checked against skolems is not analysed, and an explicit contract
        \\is then the only way to describe it.
    );
    bullet(37, #string,oneline
        \\A loop body is re-analysed from the joined entry state until that state
        \\stops growing, and diagnostics are emitted only on a final replay at the
        \\fixed point, so a defect that needs a second iteration to appear is
        \\found. Every `continue` is a back edge and joins into the head; a `break`
        \\joins into the exit instead.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\concrete :: (x: s64) -> s64 { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\generic  :: (x: $T) -> T    { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% %\n", concrete(1), generic(2));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> both.glo:4:36
        \\  |
        \\4 | concrete :: (x: s64) -> s64 { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\  |                                    ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> both.glo:4:36
        \\  |
        \\4 | concrete :: (x: s64) -> s64 { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\  |                                    ^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> both.glo:5:36
        \\  |
        \\5 | generic  :: (x: $T) -> T    { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\  |                                    ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> both.glo:5:36
        \\  |
        \\5 | generic  :: (x: $T) -> T    { p := alloc(64); return x; }
        \\  |                                    ^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\Two warnings, one for each body. The generic is reported at the template
        \\itself and once only, however many instantiations the program makes. The
        \\chapter on the limits of the memory analysis gives the complete list of
        \\blind spots.
    );
    note(41, #string,oneline
        \\Standard-library bodies emit no temporal diagnostics into a user build
        \\unless `--borrow-check-std` is passed. Their explicit contracts are still
        \\validated and their inferred summaries still propagate to call sites;
        \\only the warnings their own statements would raise are suppressed.
        \\Without that rule every program would open with the library's own
        \\findings.
    );
}

render_section_121 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory` states facts about a function's effect on memory that the
        \\analyser cannot work out for itself. Its principal use is a declaration
        \\with no body: a `#foreign` or `#magic` function has nothing to infer
        \\from, so without a contract it contributes no facts at all.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\libc     :: #library "c";
        \\c_malloc :: (n: u64) -> ?*void #foreign libc "malloc"
        \\    #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(c_free));
        \\c_free   :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory(kills(p), noescape(p));
        \\c_puts   :: (s: *const s8) -> s32 #foreign libc "puts"
        \\    #memory(reads(s), noescape(s));
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\There are three spellings: a single clause written directly after
        \\`#memory`, a parenthesised comma-separated list, or the same list in
        \\braces, which is the spelling the standard library uses. Both list forms
        \\accept a trailing comma before the closing delimiter, and an empty list
        \\in either delimiter is accepted and states nothing. The directive may
        \\also be repeated on one declaration, in which case the effect sets
        \\concatenate, so the declaration below states exactly what the
        \\parenthesised `c_free` above states.
    );
    code_block(3, "glosso", #string
        \\c_free :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory kills(p) #memory noescape(p);
    );

    subheading(4, "The clause vocabulary");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\There are fifteen clauses and no others; an unrecognised name is
        \\`error: unknown #memory effect 'reclaims'`. Ten of them require a place.
        \\Eight of those ten describe what the call does to an argument rather
        \\than to its result, and the remaining two name the argument the result
        \\is derived from. Every one of the eight but `noescape` also accepts a
        \\`maybe_` prefix, which records that the effect may happen rather than
        \\that it does.
    );
    table_header(6, "Clause   —   Argument   —   States");
    table_row(7, "`returns_fresh`   |   optional place list   |   the result, or each place named in it, is newly created storage the caller now owns");
    table_row(8, "`returns_static`   |   optional place list   |   the result has static storage duration and needs no release");
    table_row(9, "`returns_borrow`   |   parameter, after an optional place   |   the result aliases storage reached through that parameter");
    table_row(10, "`returns_unique_borrow`   |   parameter, after an optional place   |   the result is the only reference derived from that parameter while it is live");
    table_row(11, "`released_by`   |   releaser, after an optional place and before an optional allocator instance, or `place:`, `by:` and `instance:` labelled in any order   |   the fresh result must be released by calling that function");
    table_row(12, "`leak`   |   optional place   |   the resource is deliberately never released");
    table_row(13, "`kills`   |   place list   |   the call releases the resource reached through each place");
    table_row(14, "`owns`   |   place list, then an optional `into:` destination   |   the release obligation transfers to the callee, or into the destination the caller already owns");
    table_row(15, "`gives`   |   place list, then an optional `released_by:` releaser   |   on return each place holds a fresh resource the caller must release");
    table_row(16, "`invalidates`   |   place list   |   the call may replace the place's backing storage");
    table_row(17, "`noescape`   |   place list   |   the callee keeps no reference to the place past the call");
    table_row(18, "`escapes`   |   place list, then an optional `into:` destination   |   the callee does keep one, in a named parameter, in `returned`, or in `global`");
    table_row(19, "`reads`   |   place list   |   the call reads through each place");
    table_row(20, "`writes`   |   place list   |   the call writes through each place");
    table_row(21, "`unknown`   |   optional `arg:`, `result:` and `reason:` arguments   |   the analyser is told nothing about the function, or about that argument or result");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Some combinations are refused outright. `noescape(x)` together with
        \\`escapes(x)` is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory noescape(p) conflicts with escapes(p)`;
        \\two return origins on one function are
        \\`error: [memory.general] conflicting #memory return origins 'returns_fresh' and 'returns_static'`;
        \\and `released_by` without a fresh origin is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory released_by requires a fresh returned resource`.
    );

    subheading(23, "Grammar");
    code_block(24, "ebnf", #string
        \\memory-directive := "#memory" ( effect
        \\                              | "(" [ effect-list ] ")"
        \\                              | "{" [ effect-list ] "}" )
        \\effect-list      := effect { "," effect } [ "," ]
        \\
        \\effect := "returns_fresh"         [ "(" place-list ")" ]
        \\        | "returns_static"        [ "(" place-list ")" ]
        \\        | "returns_borrow"        "(" [ place "," ] place ")"
        \\        | "returns_unique_borrow" "(" [ place "," ] place ")"
        \\        | "released_by" "(" [ place "," ] releaser [ "," place ] ")"
        \\        | "released_by" "(" labelled { "," labelled } [ "," ] ")"
        \\        | "leak"     [ "(" path ")" ]
        \\        | "noescape" "(" place-list ")"
        \\        | "unknown"  [ "(" hole-arg { "," hole-arg } ")" ]
        \\        | maybe ( "kills" | "invalidates" | "reads" | "writes" )
        \\              "(" place-list ")"
        \\        | maybe ( "owns" | "escapes" )
        \\              "(" place-list [ "," "into" ":" place ] ")"
        \\        | maybe "gives"
        \\              "(" place-list [ "," "released_by" ":" releaser ] ")"
        \\
        \\maybe      := [ "maybe_" ]          (* glued to the effect name *)
        \\labelled   := "place" ":" place | "by" ":" releaser
        \\            | "instance" ":" place
        \\hole-arg   := "arg" ":" place | "result" ":" path
        \\            | "reason" ":" string
        \\place-list := place { "," place }
        \\place      := ( "returned" | identifier | "arg" "(" integer ")" )
        \\              { "." step }
        \\path       := identifier { "." step }
        \\releaser   := path
        \\step       := identifier | integer
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\In `returns_borrow`, `returns_unique_borrow` and `released_by` a lone
        \\argument is the parameter or the releaser; when two are given the place
        \\comes first. `released_by` also takes its operands labelled, in any
        \\order, as in `released_by(place: returned.0, by: c_free, instance: h)`;
        \\the labels exist because the two-operand positional form is told apart
        \\by inspecting whether its first operand names a result place. Omitting
        \\`by:` from the labelled form is
        \\`error: #memory released_by needs by: to name the releaser`. A dotted
        \\name is a path, and it reaches into an argument as readily as into a
        \\result: `kills(cfg.buffer)` names the `buffer` field reached through the
        \\parameter `cfg`, and a path step may be a tuple element index, so
        \\`returned.0` names the first result of a multiple-return signature.
    );

    subheading(26, "Attachment sites");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory` appears in five syntactic positions. The syntax does not
        \\distinguish them, but each accepts a different set of clauses, because a
        \\clause describing an acquisition has no meaning on a signature and a
        \\clause describing a signature has no meaning on one call.
    );
    table_header(28, "Site   —   Written   —   Accepts");
    table_row(29, "function declaration   |   in the attribute list after the signature   |   every clause except `leak`");
    table_row(30, "`#fn_ptr` type   |   after the parameter list and return type   |   every clause except `leak`; a function assigned to the type must satisfy it");
    table_row(31, "call expression   |   as a suffix on the call   |   every clause; here `released_by` creates the obligation");
    table_row(32, "variable declaration   |   after the type or the initializer   |   `leak` only");
    table_row(33, "overlay   |   `#memory Name { ... }`, at top level or in a block   |   every clause the target's own declaration accepts");
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\copy  := c_strdup(cast(*const s8) *text) #memory released_by(c_free);
        \\same  := c_strdup(cast(*const s8) *text) #memory released_by(by: c_free);
        \\table := alloc(64) #memory leak;
        \\c_puts(cast(*const s8) *text) #memory(reads(s), noescape(s));
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Putting a clause in the wrong position is a contract error whose message
        \\names the position it belongs to: `#memory leak` on a signature or on a
        \\`#fn_ptr` type is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory leak is only valid on an acquisition expression or variable declaration`,
        \\and anything but `leak` on a binding is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory reads is not valid on a variable declaration`
        \\with
        \\`note: only #memory leak may annotate an owning variable declaration`. A
        \\call-site contract also needs a statically resolved callee, so
        \\annotating a call made through a `#fn_ptr` value is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory on a call requires a statically resolved function`;
        \\the contract belongs on the `#fn_ptr` type instead, and a function
        \\assigned to that type is checked against it. Overlays are the subject of
        \\the chapter on overlays and foreign bindings.
    );

    subheading(36, "`arg(N)` and return places");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\`arg(N)` names the N-th parameter counting from zero. It exists for
        \\declarations whose parameter names are machine-generated or absent, and
        \\it is accepted anywhere a parameter name is. An index that does not
        \\exist produces the same shape of message a misspelled name does:
        \\`[memory.contract] #memory noescape names unknown parameter 'arg(3)' of 'f'`.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\c_free :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free"
        \\    #memory(kills(arg(0)), noescape(arg(0)));
    );
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\A place selects one variant of a tagged-union result, one element of a
        \\tuple result, or one named result of a multiple-return signature,
        \\instead of the whole result. The root `returned` is contextual syntax
        \\standing for the unnamed result; it never enters the identifier
        \\namespace. With named results the root is the result's own name, and an
        \\unnamed tuple element is reached by its index, as in `returned.0`.
    );
    code_block(40, "glosso", #string
        \\Open :: union { Ok: *u8; Err: s64; }
        \\
        \\xclose :: (f: *u8) #foreign libc "close_thing" #memory(kills(f), noescape(f));
        \\xopen  :: () -> Open #foreign libc "open_thing"
        \\    #memory(returns_fresh(returned.Ok), released_by(returned.Ok, xclose));
        \\
        \\good :: () {
        \\    r := xopen();
        \\    if r == { case .Ok(f); xclose(f); case .Err(e); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\bad :: () {
        \\    r := xopen();
        \\    if r == { case .Ok(f); _ := f.*; case .Err(e); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(41, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Ok' may not be released by 'xclose' on every path
        \\   --> open.glo:15:10
        \\   |
        \\15 |     r := xopen();
        \\   |          ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\   --> open.glo:15:10
        \\   |
        \\15 |     r := xopen();
        \\   |          ^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\`good` is clean and only `bad` is reported, because the obligation
        \\belongs to the `.Ok` variant and the `.Err` arm acquires nothing. A place
        \\naming no such variant is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory return place 'returned.Nope' names unknown union variant 'Nope'`,
        \\and a root that is neither `returned` nor a declared result name is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory return place 'x.Ok' must start with contextual 'returned'`.
    );

    subheading(43, "When a contract is an assertion");
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\On a declaration with no body a contract is taken on trust, and nothing
        \\in the compiler can contradict it. On a function that has a body the
        \\contract is also an assertion: the analyser infers a summary from the
        \\body and compares the two. An explicit return origin still wins over the
        \\inferred one, but the disagreement is reported.
    );
    code_block(45, "glosso", #string
        \\libc   :: #library "c";
        \\c_free :: (p: *u8) #foreign libc "free" #memory(kills(p), noescape(p));
        \\d_free :: (p: *u8) #foreign libc "d_free" #memory(kills(p), noescape(p));
        \\raw    :: () -> *u8 #foreign libc "raw" #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(c_free));
        \\
        \\make :: () -> *u8 #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(d_free)) {
        \\    return raw();
        \\}
    );
    code_block(46, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.contract] body of 'make' returns a resource released by 'c_free', but its #memory contract requires 'd_free'
        \\  --> wrap.glo:6:42
        \\  |
        \\6 | make :: () -> *u8 #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(d_free)) {
        \\  |                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    note(47, #string,oneline
        \\The sibling messages are
        \\`[memory.contract] body of 'f' contradicts #memory noescape(p)`, raised
        \\when the body stores the parameter where it can outlive the call, and
        \\`[memory.contract] body of 'f' contradicts its #memory return-origin contract`,
        \\raised when a `returns_static` function returns a fresh allocation. Each
        \\has a place-selected form, as in
        \\`[memory.contract] body of 'make' returns returned.Some with releaser 'free', but its #memory contract requires 'c_free'`,
        \\and an effect the body performs but the contract omits is reported
        \\separately, as in
        \\`[memory.I5] body of 'f' has undeclared escapes effect on 'p'`. All of
        \\them are temporal diagnostics, so `--borrow-check off` silences them; the
        \\clause errors elsewhere in this chapter are not, and it does not.
    );
}

render_section_122 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A resource is a value the analyser has been told must be handed back,
        \\and a releaser is the function that hands it back. Together they form an
        \\obligation attached to the expression that created the resource. The
        \\obligation is discharged by calling the releaser on that resource, and
        \\reporting the paths where it is not is the analysis's main work.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\An obligation is created in four ways: by `released_by` on the
        \\declaration of the producing function, by `released_by(f)` written on
        \\one call, by the built-in rule for dynamic arrays, and by a call whose
        \\return origin is unknown, which acquires a resource whose releaser
        \\prints as `<unknown releaser>`. The older call-site spelling
        \\`resource(released_by: f)` was removed; writing it is
        \\`error: #memory resource(...) was removed; use released_by(...) on the acquisition`.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    print("%\n", values.count);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\That program warns. The declaration `values: [..]s64` carries no
        \\contract of its own and creates nothing; the `array_add` call is what
        \\makes the array owned, because every such call attaches a release
        \\requirement naming `array_free`. A dynamic array that is declared and
        \\never grown owns no storage and is not reported.
    );

    subheading(4, "Discharging with `defer`");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\`defer` is the ordinary way to discharge an obligation, and the analyser
        \\models it exactly: deferred statements run at scope exit and at every
        \\`return`, in reverse order of registration. Writing the release
        \\immediately after the acquisition therefore covers every exit from the
        \\block, including the exits added later.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\make_buffer :: (n: ssize) -> ?*void #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(free)) {
        \\    return alloc(n);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b := make_buffer(64);
        \\    defer free(b);
        \\    print("owned by main\n");
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\Because the order is reverse registration, two `defer` statements that
        \\both touch the resource must be written with the release last. Written
        \\the other way round the release runs first, and the read that follows it
        \\is a use after release reported at the deferred statement rather than at
        \\the closing brace.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    defer print("%\n", values[0]);
        \\    defer array_free(*values);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.use_after_release] use after release while reading memory
        \\  --> order.glo:6:24
        \\  |
        \\6 |     defer print("%\n", values[0]);
        \\  |                        ^^^^^^
        \\note: resource was released here
        \\  --> order.glo:7:11
        \\  |
        \\7 |     defer array_free(*values);
        \\  |           ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> order.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
    );
    note(10, #string,oneline
        \\The analyser follows both continuations of postfix `?` and applies
        \\`#try defer` cleanup when propagation exits its scope. That can discharge
        \\an obligation on the failure path; success still needs a release or an
        \\ownership transfer. An explicit `return .Err(...)` does not run try-only
        \\cleanup. Use plain `defer` for a release required on every exit.
    );

    subheading(11, "Conditional release");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\At a branch join a resource that is alive on one path and released on
        \\the other becomes maybe-dead, and the wording weakens from a definite
        \\claim to a possible one. The two forms are worth telling apart: the
        \\first says every path leaks, the second says at least one does.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\work :: (flag: bool) {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    if flag { free(p); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    work(true);
        \\    print("done\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> sometimes.glo:5:10
        \\  |
        \\5 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> sometimes.glo:5:10
        \\  |
        \\5 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^
        \\note: one path releases it here; another path reaches the scope exit without a release
        \\  --> sometimes.glo:6:15
        \\  |
        \\6 |     if flag { free(p); }
        \\  |               ^^^^
    );

    subheading(15, "Obligations on aggregate members");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\Ownership reaches into members. A struct, union, array, or tuple field
        \\that acquires a resource carries its own obligation, and the diagnostic
        \\points at the argument expression that reached the member rather than at
        \\the binding, so the call that made the field an owner is the one named.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Holder :: struct { values: [..]s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Holder;
        \\    array_add(*a.values, 1);
        \\    print("%\n", a.values.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'array_free' before leaving scope
        \\  --> holder.glo:7:15
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_add(*a.values, 1);
        \\  |               ^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> holder.glo:7:15
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_add(*a.values, 1);
        \\  |               ^
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The remedy is a releaser for the aggregate. A `kills(p)` contract kills
        \\every place reachable through the argument, so one call discharges the
        \\obligations of all the members at once, and the aggregate itself becomes
        \\the resource a caller has to account for.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Buffer :: struct { values: [..]s64; label: string; }
        \\
        \\buffer_free :: (b: *Buffer) #memory(kills(b), reads(b), noescape(b)) {
        \\    array_free(*b.*.values);
        \\}
        \\
        \\buffer_make :: () -> Buffer #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(buffer_free)) {
        \\    b: Buffer #memory leak;
        \\    b.label = "counts";
        \\    return b;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b := buffer_make();
        \\    defer buffer_free(*b);
        \\    array_add(*b.values, 7);
        \\    print("% %\n", b.label, b.values.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\counts 1
    );

    subheading(22, "Transfer through a return");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Returning a resource transfers the obligation to the caller. A function
        \\that acquires and returns is not reported; its caller is, at the call
        \\expression, which is where the resource enters a scope that could
        \\release it.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\make_buffer :: (n: ssize) -> ?*void {
        \\    return alloc(n);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b := make_buffer(64);
        \\    print("made\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> owner.glo:9:10
        \\  |
        \\9 |     b := make_buffer(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> owner.glo:9:10
        \\  |
        \\9 |     b := make_buffer(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\That worked because `make_buffer` carries no contract at all, so the
        \\releaser was inferred from its body. Writing any explicit return origin
        \\draws an ownership boundary instead: a bare `#memory returns_fresh` says
        \\the result is fresh and declines to say who releases it, and the
        \\callee's releaser is not inherited. Adding that one clause to the
        \\example silences the call site completely, which is why a
        \\`returns_fresh` contract is normally paired with `released_by`.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Two different obligations on one resource are a conflict, not an
        \\accumulation. A value that arrives with one required releaser and then
        \\meets an operation demanding another is reported at the operation.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\my_free :: (a: *[..]s64) #memory(kills(a), noescape(a)) { array_free(a); }
        \\
        \\make :: () -> [..]s64 #memory(returns_fresh, released_by(my_free)) {
        \\    v: [..]s64 #memory leak;
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    v := make();
        \\    array_add(*v, 1);
        \\    my_free(*v);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.wrong_releaser] resource requires release by 'my_free', but this operation requires 'array_free'
        \\   --> mismatch.glo:12:5
        \\   |
        \\12 |     array_add(*v, 1);
        \\   |     ^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\   --> mismatch.glo:11:10
        \\   |
        \\11 |     v := make();
        \\   |          ^^^^
    );

    subheading(30, "Deliberate leaks");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory leak` marks a resource that is never released on purpose: an
        \\interned table, a global cache, anything whose lifetime is the process.
        \\It is the only clause accepted on a variable declaration, and on an
        \\aggregate binding it covers every member place reached from that name.
        \\It removes the obligation and changes nothing else, so an enabled debug
        \\allocator still reports the allocation when the process ends.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Holder :: struct { values: [..]s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Holder #memory leak;
        \\    array_add(*c.values, 3);
        \\    print("%\n", c.values.count);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\A binding with neither an initializer nor an aggregate type owns
        \\nothing, so the annotation is refused: `x: s64 #memory leak;` is
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory leak requires an owned resource` with
        \\`note: an uninitialized scalar or pointer binding has no allocation to leak`.
    );

    subheading(34, "The obligation diagnostics");
    table_header(35, "Message   —   Raised when");
    table_row(36, "`resource must be released by 'f' before leaving scope`   |   the obligation is live on every path out of the scope");
    table_row(37, "`resource may not be released by 'f' on every path`   |   it is live on some paths and discharged on others");
    table_row(38, "`resource in 'returned.Ok' must be released by 'f' before leaving scope`   |   the same pair of forms, for an obligation on a return place");
    table_row(39, "`double release of resource`   |   a release reaches a resource already dead on every path");
    table_row(40, "`resource may already have been released`   |   a release reaches one that is dead on some paths only");
    table_row(41, "`use after release while reading memory`   |   a read reaches released storage");
    table_row(42, "`resource requires release by 'A', but this operation requires 'B'`   |   two operations demand different releasers for one resource");
    table_row(43, "`resource requires release by 'A', but 'B' was called`   |   a release call names a releaser the resource does not require");
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\Each carries a `note` locating the origin of the resource, and the
        \\release-related ones a second note at the release that already happened.
        \\The on-every-path form carries a third note, `one path releases it
        \\here; another path reaches the scope exit without a release`, at the
        \\release the other path performs. The write and return variants of the
        \\use-after-release message read `while writing memory` and `while
        \\returning the value`. Every message is printed behind a bracketed rule
        \\identifier — `[memory.leak]`, `[memory.maybe_leak]`,
        \\`[memory.double_release]`, `[memory.maybe_double_release]`,
        \\`[memory.use_after_release]`, `[memory.wrong_releaser]` — which is
        \\stable and searchable.
    );
}

render_section_123 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Aliasing is modelled with a counter. Every resource carries a
        \\generation, and every reference to it records the generation it observed
        \\when it was made. A reference whose recorded generation is behind the
        \\resource's current one is stale, and using it is reported.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\`invalidates(p)` is what advances the counter. It says the call may
        \\replace the storage reached through `p`, so any address derived from the
        \\old storage is now meaningless. The clause exists because that is what a
        \\growing container does: reallocating the backing block leaves the
        \\descriptor valid and every view of the old block dangling.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\array_add   :: (a: *[..]$T, value: ...T) #magic "array_add"
        \\    #memory{invalidates(a), writes(a), noescape(a), owns(value, into: a)};
        \\array_free  :: (a: *[..]$T) #magic "array_free"
        \\    #memory{kills(a), noescape(a)};
        \\array_view  :: (a: [..]$T) -> []T #magic "array_view"
        \\    #memory{returns_borrow(a), reads(a), noescape(a)};
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Those three prelude declarations are the whole model in miniature:
        \\growing invalidates and takes ownership of what it stores, freeing
        \\kills, taking a view borrows. A view taken
        \\before a growth and read after it is exactly the case the generation
        \\counter exists to catch.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    view := array_view(values);
        \\    array_add(*values, 2);
        \\    print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\    array_free(*values);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(5, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.stale_reference] stale reference used after storage invalidation
        \\  --> views.glo:8:18
        \\  |
        \\8 |     print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\  |                  ^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> views.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: storage was invalidated here
        \\  --> views.glo:7:5
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_add(*values, 2);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^
    );

    subheading(6, "Why the owner survives");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\Only `view` is reported. The second `array_add` and the `array_free`
        \\after it both go through `values`, and both are clean, even though the
        \\same call raised the generation. When a call invalidates a parameter the
        \\analyser bumps the generation and then refreshes the root expression
        \\that was passed as that argument.
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\The reason is that the owner is the thing being updated. `array_add`
        \\writes the new block's address back into the descriptor it was handed,
        \\so `values` is current by construction while every reference derived
        \\earlier from the old block is not. Refreshing exactly the argument root
        \\encodes that asymmetry without the analyser needing to know anything
        \\about the container's layout.
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\A reference made after the invalidation observes the new generation and
        \\is current. The program below compiles with no diagnostics and prints
        \\`1 2`: `old` is stale but never used, and `fresh` is fine.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    old := array_view(values);
        \\    array_add(*values, 2);
        \\    fresh := array_view(values);
        \\    print("% %\n", fresh[0], fresh[1]);
        \\    array_free(*values);
        \\}
    );
    note(11, #string,oneline
        \\The refresh follows the argument expression, not the type. A pointer
        \\copied out of the owner before the call, such as `alias := *values`, is
        \\a separate reference and does go stale, even though it has exactly the
        \\type `array_add` expects. An alias of an owner is not an owner.
    );

    subheading(12, "Releasing through a stale reference");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\A stale reference is as wrong for a release as it is for a read, and it
        \\has its own message, because the failure differs: the storage handed to
        \\the releaser is the block that was already replaced.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    alias := *values;
        \\    array_add(*values, 2);
        \\    array_free(alias);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.stale_reference] stale reference used after storage invalidation
        \\  --> alias.glo:8:5
        \\  |
        \\8 |     array_free(alias);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> alias.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: storage was invalidated here
        \\  --> alias.glo:7:5
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_add(*values, 2);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.stale_release] release through a stale reference
        \\  --> alias.glo:8:5
        \\  |
        \\8 |     array_free(alias);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: storage was invalidated here
        \\  --> alias.glo:7:5
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_add(*values, 2);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^
    );

    subheading(16, "Conditional invalidation");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\When the invalidating call sits inside a branch, the join leaves the
        \\reference stale on one path and current on the other, and the wording
        \\weakens accordingly. The note still points at the call that did it.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    view := array_view(values);
        \\    if values.count == 1 { array_add(*values, 2); }
        \\    print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\    array_free(*values);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_stale_reference] reference may be stale after conditional invalidation
        \\  --> maybe.glo:8:18
        \\  |
        \\8 |     print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\  |                  ^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> maybe.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: storage was invalidated here
        \\  --> maybe.glo:7:28
        \\  |
        \\7 |     if values.count == 1 { array_add(*values, 2); }
        \\  |                            ^^^^^^^^^
    );

    subheading(20, "Borrows");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`returns_borrow(p)` is the clause that makes a result share a resource
        \\with a parameter instead of owning one. The result acquires no
        \\obligation of its own and inherits the resource's fate: releasing the
        \\owner turns a later read through the borrow into a use after release
        \\rather than a stale-reference report, because the storage is gone
        \\instead of moved.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    view := array_view(values);
        \\    array_free(*values);
        \\    print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.use_after_release] use after release while reading memory
        \\  --> freed.glo:8:18
        \\  |
        \\8 |     print("%\n", view[0]);
        \\  |                  ^^^^
        \\note: resource was released here
        \\  --> freed.glo:7:5
        \\  |
        \\7 |     array_free(*values);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> freed.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\A `returns_borrow` borrow is a link from a result to a parameter and
        \\nothing more. There is no notation for a lifetime and no reborrowing
        \\relation: passing a view on to another function does not create a
        \\derived borrow tracked separately, and no rule requires such a borrow to
        \\end before the owner is next mutated. What is caught is the use, at the
        \\point of use. `returns_unique_borrow` is the exception: it does have an
        \\extent, and a second unique borrow taken while the first is still read
        \\later is `warning: [memory.X2] a second unique borrow overlaps the
        \\first`, with `note: first unique borrow created here` at the earlier
        \\one.
    );
    table_header(25, "Clause   —   Effect on the analysis");
    table_row(26, "`invalidates(p)`   |   raises the resource's generation and refreshes the argument root");
    table_row(27, "`returns_borrow(p)`   |   the result references the resource behind `p` and owns nothing");
    table_row(28, "`returns_unique_borrow(p)`   |   the same, and a second such borrow taken while the first is still live is reported");
    table_row(29, "`reads(p)`, `writes(p)`   |   the argument is checked for liveness and staleness at the call");
    table_row(30, "`noescape(p)`   |   the callee keeps no reference past the call");
    table_row(31, "`escapes(p)`   |   the callee keeps one, so a local address passed in is reported as escaping");

    subheading(32, "`noalias`");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`noalias` is a parameter modifier, not a `#memory` clause. It is written
        \\before the parameter name, it is valid nowhere else, and it lowers
        \\directly to the LLVM `noalias` attribute on that argument. It asserts
        \\that the storage reached through this parameter is reached through no
        \\other parameter for the duration of the call.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\fill :: (noalias a: *s64, noalias b: *s64) -> s64 {
        \\    a.* = 1;
        \\    b.* = 2;
        \\    return a.*;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    x: s64 = 0;
        \\    print("%\n", fill(*x, *x));
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\The assertion is checked, but only where the overlap is visible in the
        \\caller. The program above passes one address twice and is `warning:
        \\[memory.X1] noalias argument overlaps another live reference` at the
        \\call, at every optimization level; `--borrow-check off` silences it and
        \\`--borrow-check error` raises it to an error like any other temporal
        \\finding. Under the default the build proceeds, and the program still
        \\answers differently depending on the optimization level: `2` at `-O0`,
        \\where the load actually happens, and `1` at `-O1` and `-O2`, where the
        \\optimizer is entitled to assume the store through `b` cannot have
        \\touched `a`. A body that retains a `noalias` parameter is reported
        \\separately as `warning: [memory.X3] noalias parameter 'a' is retained
        \\by this call`.
    );
    note(36, #string,oneline
        \\`noalias` is the one memory annotation in the language that can change
        \\the result of a correct-looking program. `#memory` contracts affect
        \\diagnostics only; a wrong `noalias` affects code generation. Write it
        \\where the caller's contract genuinely forbids overlap, and nowhere else.
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\The modifier is not part of the parameter's type for overload purposes:
        \\two declarations differing only in `noalias` are
        \\`error: function 'f' is already declared`. Repeating it on one parameter
        \\is `error: duplicate noalias parameter modifier`, and writing it on a
        \\local binding does not parse at all.
    );
}

render_section_124 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An overlay supplies a `#memory` contract for a function whose
        \\declaration cannot be edited. It is written `#memory <target> {
        \\<effects> }`, names the function it describes, and merges its
        \\effects into that function's contract exactly as if they had been
        \\written in the declaration's own attribute list. The clause
        \\vocabulary is the one given in the chapter on writing `#memory`
        \\contracts; an overlay adds no new effects, only a second place to
        \\put them.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Overlays exist because the signature and the ownership rule usually
        \\come from different places. A `#foreign` declaration states a C
        \\signature, which is all a header can tell the compiler; whether the
        \\returned pointer is owned, and which function releases it, is a
        \\fact about the library that no header records. Generated bindings
        \\make the gap unavoidable: `bindgen` reads access modes out of the C
        \\signature — `reads(p)` for a `const` pointee, `writes(p)` otherwise —
        \\but it has nothing to say about ownership, so a pointer-returning
        \\function is emitted with `#memory unknown(result: returned)` and a
        \\function with no pointers at all with `#memory()`.
    );
    subheading(2, "The blanket-unknown problem");
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\`unknown` sets a flag on the function's summary and makes the
        \\result an opaque value carrying a resource whose releaser is not
        \\known. The obligation is reported, but nothing can discharge it and
        \\no release through such a binding is tracked, so a program built on
        \\blanket-`unknown` bindings gets a leak report and no misuse analysis.
        \\This program contains a real double free.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\libc     :: #library "c";
        \\c_malloc :: (n: u64) -> ?*void #foreign libc "malloc" #memory unknown;
        \\c_free   :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory unknown;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := c_malloc(64);
        \\    c_free(p);
        \\    c_free(p);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Nothing is reported about the second release. What is reported is an
        \\obligation nobody can discharge and the advisory that fires for every
        \\opaque foreign call with no return origin; `unknown` does not silence
        \\the advisory, it only changes the note, which then recommends three
        \\clauses that do not include `unknown`.
    );
    code_block(6, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource may not be released by '<unknown releaser>' on every path
        \\  --> ...\ex1.glo:6:10
        \\  |
        \\6 |     p := c_malloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> ...\ex1.glo:6:10
        \\  |
        \\6 |     p := c_malloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.unknown_lifetime] lifetime of memory returned by opaque foreign call 'c_malloc' is unknown
        \\  --> ...\ex1.glo:6:10
        \\  |
        \\6 |     p := c_malloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^
        \\note: add returns_fresh, returns_borrow(...), or returns_static to its #memory contract
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The build succeeds and the program aborts with the Windows
        \\heap-corruption status `0xc0000374`. Two overlays turn the same
        \\defect into a compile-time diagnostic without touching either
        \\declaration.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\libc     :: #library "c";
        \\c_malloc :: (n: u64) -> ?*void #foreign libc "malloc" #memory unknown;
        \\c_free   :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory unknown;
        \\
        \\#memory c_malloc { returns_fresh, released_by(c_free), };
        \\#memory c_free   { kills(arg(0)), noescape(arg(0)), };
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := c_malloc(64);
        \\    c_free(p);
        \\    c_free(p);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.double_release] double release of resource
        \\   --> ...\ex2.glo:11:5
        \\   |
        \\11 |     c_free(p);
        \\   |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: first release occurred here
        \\   --> ...\ex2.glo:10:5
        \\   |
        \\10 |     c_free(p);
        \\   |     ^^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.use_after_release] use after release while writing memory
        \\   --> ...\ex2.glo:11:5
        \\   |
        \\11 |     c_free(p);
        \\   |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: resource was released here
        \\   --> ...\ex2.glo:10:5
        \\   |
        \\10 |     c_free(p);
        \\   |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: resource originated here
        \\  --> ...\ex2.glo:9:10
        \\  |
        \\9 |     p := c_malloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\Deleting the second `c_free` makes the program compile with no
        \\diagnostics at all: the overlay on `c_malloc` also satisfied the
        \\opaque-return advisory, because the return origin is no longer
        \\unknown.
    );
    subheading(11, "The two placements");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The same syntax means two different things depending on where it is
        \\written. At top level an overlay is a program-wide amendment to the
        \\target's contract; inside a body it is a statement whose effect
        \\lasts to the end of the enclosing block.
    );
    table_header(13, "Placement   —   Reach   —   Applied by");
    table_row(14, "Top level   |   The whole program; every call to the target sees it.   |   Concatenation onto the declaration's own effect list.");
    table_row(15, "Statement in a body   |   From the statement to the end of the enclosing block.   |   A separate summary, checked against the declaration's contract for conflicts.");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\Top-level overlays are collected from the whole program before any
        \\analysis runs, so they may be written before or after the
        \\declaration they amend, and in a different file. The natural place
        \\for them is one hand-written file next to the generated bindings.
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The effect list is comma-separated and a trailing comma before `}`
        \\is accepted, which is what lets a generator emit one clause per
        \\line. An empty list is `error: #memory overlay effect list cannot be
        \\empty`; the semicolon after the closing brace is optional.
    );
    subheading(18, "Block-scoped overlays");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The statement form is for a local convention: a region of code that
        \\knows more about a call than the program as a whole does. Outside
        \\the block the declaration reverts to whatever contract it carries.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\libc       :: #library "c";
        \\opaque_get :: (n: u64) -> ?*void #foreign libc "malloc" #memory unknown;
        \\opaque_put :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory{kills(p), noescape(p)};
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    {
        \\        #memory opaque_get { returns_fresh, released_by(opaque_put) }
        \\        a := opaque_get(16);
        \\        _ := a;
        \\    }
        \\    b := opaque_get(16);
        \\    _ := b;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'opaque_put' before leaving scope
        \\  --> ...\a6.glo:8:14
        \\  |
        \\8 |         a := opaque_get(16);
        \\  |              ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> ...\a6.glo:8:14
        \\  |
        \\8 |         a := opaque_get(16);
        \\  |              ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by '<unknown releaser>' before leaving scope
        \\   --> ...\a6.glo:11:10
        \\   |
        \\11 |     b := opaque_get(16);
        \\   |          ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\   --> ...\a6.glo:11:10
        \\   |
        \\11 |     b := opaque_get(16);
        \\   |          ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.unknown_lifetime] lifetime of memory returned by opaque foreign call 'opaque_get' is unknown
        \\   --> ...\a6.glo:11:10
        \\   |
        \\11 |     b := opaque_get(16);
        \\   |          ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: add returns_fresh, returns_borrow(...), or returns_static to its #memory contract
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The three diagnostics are the whole story: inside the block the call
        \\creates a release obligation naming `opaque_put` and leaks it, and
        \\outside the block the same call falls back to an obligation with no
        \\nameable releaser, plus the advisory.
    );
    subheading(23, "Naming a parameter that has no usable name");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Every clause that takes a parameter accepts either an identifier or
        \\`arg(N)`, the zero-based position of the parameter in the
        \\declaration. Generated declarations frequently have names taken from
        \\a header, renamed, or absent, so `arg(N)` is the spelling a
        \\machine-written overlay uses. An index past the end of the parameter
        \\list is `error: [memory.contract] #memory kills names unknown parameter
        \\'arg(3)' of 'c_free'`.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\Texture       :: struct { id: u32; }
        \\gfx           :: #library "c";
        \\LoadTexture   :: (name: string) -> Texture #foreign gfx "LoadTexture" #memory unknown;
        \\UnloadTexture :: (t: Texture) #foreign gfx "UnloadTexture" #memory unknown;
        \\
        \\#memory LoadTexture   { returns_fresh, released_by(UnloadTexture), noescape(arg(0)), };
        \\#memory UnloadTexture { kills(arg(0)), noescape(arg(0)), };
        \\
        \\leaking :: () {
        \\    t := LoadTexture("wall.png");
        \\    _ := t.id;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'UnloadTexture' before leaving scope
        \\   --> ...\a4.glo:10:10
        \\   |
        \\10 |     t := LoadTexture("wall.png");
        \\   |          ^^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\   --> ...\a4.glo:10:10
        \\   |
        \\10 |     t := LoadTexture("wall.png");
        \\   |          ^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A `Texture` is a struct of one `u32`, not a pointer, and the
        \\analysis still tracks it. Named aggregates are treated as
        \\memory-capable unconditionally, which is what makes handle types
        \\like this one contractable.
    );
    subheading(28, "Resolving and validating the target");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\The target name must select exactly one function declaration in the
        \\program. A name that selects none is `error: [memory.contract] #memory
        \\overlay names unknown function 'no_such_function'`, and a name that
        \\selects more than one is `error: [memory.contract] #memory overlay
        \\target 'f' is ambiguous; select one overload`. Both are reported
        \\before any analysis runs, and like every other contract error they are
        \\fatal at every `--borrow-check` setting.
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The merged contract is then validated exactly like a written one, so
        \\an overlay is subject to every rule in the contract validator: a
        \\releaser must exist, must declare `kills(...)` or `owns(...)` on a
        \\memory-capable parameter, and must release on every path — a releaser
        \\whose only release effect is a `maybe_` one is refused; `released_by`
        \\requires a fresh return, `noescape(p)` and `escapes(p)` on the same
        \\parameter conflict, and a return place must name a real variant of a
        \\tagged-union result.
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\An overlay is an assertion, not a check. The compiler has no body to
        \\compare it against and does not verify the claim; a wrong overlay
        \\silently teaches the analysis a wrong fact, which is worse than no
        \\overlay at all. Write one only for a function whose ownership rule
        \\is documented.
    );
    subheading(32, "Call-site contracts");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\A `#memory` directive may also be attached to a single call
        \\expression, after the argument list. This is the narrowest scope of
        \\all: one call. It accepts the whole vocabulary, and here
        \\`released_by` does not describe an obligation but creates one.
    );
    table_header(34, "Spelling   —   Meaning at a call site");
    table_row(35, "`#memory released_by(f)`   |   This call produced a resource that `f` releases.");
    table_row(36, "`#memory leak`   |   This resource is deliberately never released.");
    table_row(37, "`#memory kills(p)`, `reads(p)`, `noescape(p)`   |   An effect overlay applied to this one call.");
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\libc       :: #library "c";
        \\opaque_get :: (n: u64) -> ?*void #foreign libc "malloc" #memory unknown;
        \\opaque_put :: (p: ?*void) #foreign libc "free" #memory(kills(p), noescape(p));
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := opaque_get(64) #memory released_by(opaque_put);
        \\    opaque_put(p);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\That program compiles clean. Removing the `opaque_put` call gives
        \\`warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'opaque_put'
        \\before leaving scope`, pointing at the acquisition. `#memory leak`
        \\cannot stand alone on such a call: without a releaser there is no
        \\obligation to waive, and the compiler says `error: [memory.contract]
        \\#memory leak requires an owned result with a known releaser`, with the
        \\note `add released_by(...) to the acquisition contract or remove
        \\#memory leak`.
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\A call-site contract may refine the declaration's contract but never
        \\contradict it. Naming a different releaser is `error: [memory.general]
        \\call-site #memory releaser 'c_free2' conflicts with function releaser
        \\'c_free'`; claiming `escapes(p)` where the declaration says
        \\`noescape(p)` is `error: [memory.general] call-site #memory escape
        \\facts conflict with the function contract`.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\The callee must be statically known. Calling through a function
        \\pointer gives `error: [memory.contract] #memory on a call requires a
        \\statically resolved function`, because the analyser has no single
        \\summary to amend.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\The directive attaches only to a call. Writing it on any other
        \\expression, as in `x := 1 + 2 #memory returns_fresh;`, gives
        \\`error: [memory.contract] #memory on an expression is only supported
        \\on a function call`.
    );
    subheading(43, "Silencing the escape advisory");
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\The second advisory raised for opaque foreign calls concerns
        \\arguments rather than results: passing the address of a local to a
        \\callee whose contract does not say what it does with the pointer
        \\gives `warning: [memory.maybe_dangling_escape] pointer to local
        \\storage may escape through opaque foreign call 'c_puts'`, with a note
        \\locating the local and the note `add #memory noescape(s) if the callee
        \\does not retain it`. An overlay is how that is answered for a
        \\generated declaration.
    );
    code_block(45, "glosso", #string
        \\libc   :: #library "c";
        \\c_puts :: (s: *const s8) -> s32 #foreign libc "puts" #memory unknown;
        \\
        \\#memory c_puts { reads(arg(0)), noescape(arg(0)), };
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    buffer: [8]s8;
        \\    buffer[0] = 66;
        \\    buffer[1] = 0;
        \\    c_puts(cast(*const s8) *buffer);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(46, #string,oneline
        \\Without the overlay the advisory fires; with it the program compiles
        \\silently. `escapes(arg(0))` does not silence it, and is not meant
        \\to: it replaces the conditional warning with the definite `warning:
        \\[memory.escape] pointer escapes through this call`. The overlay states
        \\which of the two is true.
    );
}

render_section_125 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\The memory pass is an abstract interpreter over the checked program.
        \\It runs after type checking and before lowering, computes a summary
        \\for every function, and walks each body once against those
        \\summaries. It is deliberately an approximation, and it approximates
        \\in the unsound direction: a clean build is not a proof that a
        \\program is free of leaks, double frees, or dangling pointers.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\This chapter is the list of places where the analysis is known to
        \\be wrong. Some are false negatives: the pass says nothing where
        \\something is. Others report a defect in a correct program.
        \\Both directions matter, because a warning that cannot be
        \\true trains a reader to ignore the ones that can.
    );
    table_header(2, "Blind spot   —   What is missed");
    table_row(3, "Generic bodies   |   Their own defects are reported, but no effect is inferred for their callers, so a correct caller is diagnosed as leaking.");
    table_row(4, "`unknown` contracts   |   Every release through the call; the result carries an obligation nothing can discharge.");
    table_row(5, "Custom allocator behavior   |   Arbitrary allocator procedures and direct context-field replacement do not establish tracked temporary origins.");
    table_row(6, "Standard-library bodies   |   Diagnostics are suppressed inside them unless `--borrow-check-std` is passed.");
    subheading(7, "Generic effects do not reach callers");
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\A function with a type parameter is walked like any other, and its
        \\own defects are reported. What is not produced is a summary: no
        \\effect is inferred for a generic body, so nothing it does reaches
        \\its callers. The two functions below differ only in the parameter's
        \\type, and both are reported.
    );
    code_block(9, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\concrete :: (x: s64) -> s64 {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    return x;
        \\}
        \\
        \\generic :: (x: $T) -> T {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    return x;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    _ := concrete(1);
        \\    _ := generic(1);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> ...\b1.glo:5:10
        \\  |
        \\5 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> ...\b1.glo:5:10
        \\  |
        \\5 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\  |          ^^^^^
        \\
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\   --> ...\b1.glo:10:10
        \\   |
        \\10 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\   |          ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\   --> ...\b1.glo:10:10
        \\   |
        \\10 |     p := alloc(64);
        \\   |          ^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\Two warnings, one per body. A double free written in either position
        \\is reported the same way.
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The hole is on the other side of the call. A generic wrapper reports
        \\its own defects but contributes no effect to its caller, so the
        \\obligation reaches the caller unsatisfied and a correct program is
        \\diagnosed as leaking. `--memory-signatures` prints the wrapper below
        \\as `gen_free :: #memory()`.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\gen_free :: (p: $T) { free(p); }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    gen_free(p);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\That is `warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may
        \\not be released by 'free' on every path`, on a program with no leak.
        \\Writing the contract out does not fix it. Because nothing is inferred
        \\for the body, `gen_free :: (p: $T) #memory(kills(p), noescape(p)) {
        \\free(p); }` is `warning: [memory.I5] #memory kills(p) over-claims an
        \\every-path release in 'gen_free'` at the contract and `warning:
        \\[memory.wrong_releaser] resource requires release by 'free', but
        \\'gen_free' was called` at the call. The same wrapper written with a
        \\concrete parameter type is clean with or without a contract.
    );
    note(15, #string,oneline
        \\A generic function that acquires or releases memory has no spelling
        \\that satisfies both its own body check and its callers. The standard
        \\library's `array_add`, `array_free`, and `array_view` avoid the
        \\problem by being `#magic` declarations with hand-written contracts
        \\and no Glosso body.
    );


    subheading(16, "`unknown` and the contracts that are not there");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\`#memory unknown` records that nothing is known. A call to an
        \\`unknown` function produces an opaque value carrying a resource whose
        \\releaser prints as `<unknown releaser>`: the obligation is reported
        \\and nothing can discharge it, no release through such a binding is
        \\tracked, and no misuse is reported. The chapter on overlays and
        \\foreign bindings shows the fix; the point here is that a
        \\bindgen-generated program is, by default, unanalysed with respect to
        \\ownership, and noisy about it.
    );
    subheading(18, "`#try defer` and propagation exits");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\A plain `defer` is replayed at every scope exit, including `return`.
        \\A `#try defer` runs only when propagation leaves its scope. The analyser
        \\tracks that failure path, applies the cleanup, and preserves ownership
        \\carried by the completed failure result. An explicit error return still
        \\skips try-only cleanup, as the second procedure below demonstrates.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\E :: enum { Bad; }
        \\
        \\plain :: (fail: bool) -> E!s64 {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    defer free(p);
        \\    if fail return .Err(.Bad);
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\trying :: (fail: bool) -> E!s64 {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    #try defer free(p);
        \\    if fail return .Err(.Bad);
        \\    free(p);
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`plain` is clean. `trying` leaks when `fail` is true: its explicit
        \\`return .Err(.Bad)` skips the try-only cleanup. The possible-leak
        \\diagnostic describes that real missing release. If the failure instead
        \\arrives through `operation()?`, the `#try defer` runs and the analyser
        \\counts its release. Success still reaches the explicit `free(p)`.
    );

    subheading(22, "What is outside the analysis entirely");
    bullet(23, #string,oneline
        \\`noalias` is checked only where the overlap is visible. Passing the
        \\same address to two `noalias` parameters is `warning: [memory.X1]
        \\noalias argument overlaps another live reference`, and a body that
        \\retains one is `warning: [memory.X3] noalias parameter 'a' is
        \\retained by this call`, but neither can see an alias the caller does
        \\not spell out, and both are temporal findings, so `--borrow-check off`
        \\silences them.
    );
    bullet(24, #string,oneline
        \\`#push_allocator` selects the allocator; leaving that scope alone
        \\does not discharge an allocation's release obligation. A recognized
        \\`reset_temporary_storage` or `free_all` of that arena does discharge
        \\its tracked obligations and invalidates surviving references. Otherwise
        \\release or transfer the resource normally.
    );
    bullet(25, #string,oneline
        \\Contradictory clause pairs are accepted. `#memory(returns_borrow(p),
        \\kills(p))`, `#memory(unknown, returns_fresh)`, and a duplicated
        \\`noescape(p)` all pass the validator; only `noescape` against
        \\`escapes` on the same parameter is rejected.
    );
    bullet(26, #string,oneline
        \\Standard-library bodies emit no diagnostics into a user build. They
        \\still participate in summary inference and their written contracts
        \\are still validated, but a finding inside a standard-library
        \\implementation is suppressed unless `--borrow-check-std` is passed.
    );
    subheading(27, "Reading a false negative");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Because the failure mode is silence, the useful habit is to treat a
        \\clean memory pass as evidence about the code the pass could see. The
        \\runtime layers cover a different set: the debug allocator, enabled with
        \\a nonzero `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR`, observes actual allocations, so it
        \\catches exactly the class the static pass drops.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\gen_free :: (p: $T) { free(p); }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := alloc(64);
        \\    gen_free(p);
        \\    gen_free(p);
        \\    print("done\n");
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The memory pass reports a possible leak and never the double free,
        \\because the release happened inside a generic body whose effects do
        \\not reach `main`. With the debug allocator disabled the program can
        \\abort in the process heap, for example with `0xc0000374` on Windows.
        \\With `GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1`, the allocator names both frees and the
        \\allocation, each with the call chain that reached it.
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\$ GLOSSO_DEBUG_ALLOCATOR=1 glosso -O1 -o g1.exe g1.glo && ./g1.exe
        \\glosso debug allocator: double free
        \\    allocated at:
        \\      ...\g1.glo:7:10
        \\    first free at:
        \\      ...\g1.glo:4:23
        \\      ...\g1.glo:8:5
        \\    second free at:
        \\      ...\g1.glo:4:23
        \\      ...\g1.glo:9:5
        \\done
        \\glosso debug allocator: 1 allocator error(s)
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\The two tools are complementary and neither subsumes the other: the
        \\static pass sees every path but only through concrete bodies, and
        \\the debug allocator sees every body but only the paths that actually
        \\ran, and only allocations that went through `context.allocator`. The
        \\chapter on the debug allocator covers its full report set.
    );
}

render_section_126 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check <off|warning|error>` selects the severity of the
        \\temporal memory analysis: leaks, double releases, use after release,
        \\stale references after invalidation, escaping pointers, overlapping
        \\`noalias` arguments and unique borrows, the contract-versus-body
        \\checks, and the advisories raised for opaque foreign calls. The
        \\default is
        \\`warning`. Both spellings of the value are accepted,
        \\`--borrow-check error` and `--borrow-check=error`.
    );
    table_header(1, "Mode   —   Analysis runs   —   Effect on the build");
    table_row(2, "`off`   |   no   |   No temporal diagnostics at all; the build proceeds.");
    table_row(3, "`warning` (default)   |   yes   |   Findings print as warnings; the build proceeds and the compiler exits 0.");
    table_row(4, "`error`   |   yes   |   Findings print as errors; no artifact is produced and the compiler exits 1.");
    subheading(5, "The three modes on one leak");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\A dynamic array that is grown and never freed is the shortest real
        \\finding. The obligation comes from the `array_add` call, not from
        \\the declaration: growing an array is what makes it owned.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    values: [..]s64;
        \\    array_add(*values, 1);
        \\    array_add(*values, 2);
        \\    print("count=%\n", values.count);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\At the default setting the finding is a warning, the program is
        \\linked, and `--run` executes it.
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --run e1.glo
        \\glosso: compile e1.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\warning: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'array_free' before leaving scope
        \\  --> ...\e1.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> ...\e1.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\glosso: link e1.exe (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\glosso: done e1.exe  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\count=2
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\At `error` the identical text is printed with `error:` in place of
        \\`warning:`, and the run stops there: no link line, no artifact, no
        \\program output.
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --borrow-check error --run e1.glo
        \\glosso: compile e1.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\error: [memory.leak] resource must be released by 'array_free' before leaving scope
        \\  --> ...\e1.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> ...\e1.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\At `off` the analysis does not run and nothing is printed. The same
        \\program builds and runs exactly as it does under `warning`; the leak
        \\is still there.
    );
    subheading(13, "Exit statuses");
    table_header(14, "Situation   —   Exit status");
    table_row(15, "Warnings only, under `warning` or `off`   |   0");
    table_row(16, "Any finding under `error`   |   1");
    table_row(17, "A malformed `#memory` directive, at any mode   |   1");
    table_row(18, "A value other than `off`, `warning`, `error`   |   2");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The last of these is a driver error rather than a compile error, and
        \\prints no source frame: `glosso: borrow-check mode must be off,
        \\warning, or error`.
    );
    subheading(20, "What the flag does not cover");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check` selects the severity of the analysis. It has no
        \\effect on the contract validator, which checks that a written
        \\`#memory` directive is well formed and consistent. Contract errors
        \\are produced before the analysis is even scheduled and abort the
        \\build unconditionally.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\foo :: (x: s64) -> s64 #memory returns_fresh { return x; }
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", foo(1)); }
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --borrow-check off --backend llvm-ir -o e2.ll e2.glo
        \\glosso: compile e2.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\error: [memory.contract] #memory returns_fresh requires 'foo' to return a memory-capable value
        \\  --> ...\e2.glo:3:32
        \\  |
        \\3 | foo :: (x: s64) -> s64 #memory returns_fresh { return x; }
        \\  |                                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check off` is therefore not a way to compile a program
        \\whose contracts are wrong. It silences findings about code, never
        \\complaints about annotations.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\In the other direction, `error` promotes the whole temporal
        \\analysis, including the advisories about opaque foreign calls. A
        \\program built on bindings that carry `#memory unknown` fails to
        \\compile under `error` at the first such call, on both `error:
        \\[memory.maybe_leak] resource may not be released by '<unknown
        \\releaser>' on every path` and `error: [memory.unknown_lifetime]
        \\lifetime of memory returned by opaque foreign call 'c_malloc' is
        \\unknown`.
        \\That is the intended use: it turns "this library has no contracts
        \\yet" into a build failure rather than a page of warnings.
    );
    subheading(26, "Raising the mode in a build script");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A build script sets the mode through the `borrow_check` field of a
        \\workspace's `Build_Options`, whose type is `Compiler_Borrow_Check`
        \\with variants `Off`, `Warning`, and `Error`. The field defaults to
        \\`Warning`, matching the command line. Edit the detached options
        \\record, then install it with `set_build_options` before compiling.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Compiler";
        \\
        \\build :: () {
        \\    set_build_options_dc(.do_output = false);
        \\    workspace := compiler_create_workspace("memory-check");
        \\    options := get_build_options(workspace);
        \\    options.output_executable_name = "leaky_child.exe";
        \\    set_borrow_check(*options, Compiler_Borrow_Check.Error);
        \\    set_build_options(options, workspace);
        \\    if add_build_file("leaky_child.glo", workspace) == {
        \\    case .Ok(log);    print("[build] ok\n%", log);
        \\    case .Err(error); print("[build] memory diagnostics are fatal here\n"); exit(1);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\#comptime build();
        \\
        \\main :: () {}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\glosso: compile e6.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\[build] memory diagnostics are fatal here
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The nested compilation returns `.Err` and its diagnostics arrive in
        \\`error.message`, printed by the script rather than by the compiler.
        \\Without the `exit(1)` the outer build succeeds and returns 0: a
        \\failed inner compilation is a value, not a signal. The chapter on
        \\build scripts covers the rest of `Build_Options` and workspaces.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\The mode to choose is a project decision rather than a language one.
        \\`error` is appropriate once a program's foreign bindings all carry
        \\contracts, since from then on a new finding is almost always a new
        \\defect. `off` is appropriate for a program whose memory is managed
        \\by an arena the analysis cannot model, where the findings are known
        \\in advance to be noise.
    );

    subheading(32, "`--borrow-check-std`");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check-std` takes no value and adds the standard library's
        \\own bodies to the walk. Without it a finding inside a
        \\standard-library implementation is suppressed; with it the same run
        \\reports one at whatever severity `--borrow-check` selects, and
        \\`--borrow-check off` still silences everything. Library findings are
        \\printed among the program's own and sorted with them by path and
        \\position. The flag is for working on the library rather than on a
        \\program that uses it. The exact count changes as standard-library
        \\contracts and bodies evolve; contract-versus-body mismatches like the
        \\first one normally make up a substantial part of the listing.
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.I5] body of 'io_read_exact' has undeclared reads effect on 'destination'
        \\   --> ...\std\Io\Transfer.glo:46:13
        \\   |
        \\46 |     #memory{writes(reader, destination), noescape(reader, destination)} where Reader(R) {
        \\   |             ^^^^^^
    );
    subheading(35, "Printing the contracts");
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\Three further flags print what the analysis concluded rather than
        \\what it found. None of them changes the build: the diagnostics are
        \\unaffected and the artifact is still written.
    );
    table_header(37, "Flag   —   What it prints");
    table_row(38, "`--memory-signatures`   |   One `name :: #memory(...)` line per bodied function, after inference merged the written contract with the body's own effects.");
    table_row(39, "`--memory-axioms`   |   The same line for every bodyless declaration — `#foreign`, `#magic`, function-pointer types — normalized, with `MISSING` where no contract was written.");
    table_row(40, "`--memory-report`   |   One tab-separated row per module, counting that module's declarations against the contract kinds they carry.");
    code_block(41, "text", #string
        \\alloc :: #memory(released_by(returned.Some, free), returns_fresh(returned.Some))
        \\array_add :: #memory(invalidates(a), noescape(a), owns(value, into: a), writes(a))
        \\array_free :: #memory(kills(a), noescape(a))
        \\array_reset :: #memory(noescape(a), writes(a))
        \\array_view :: #memory(noescape(a), reads(a), returns_borrow(a))
        \\byte_slice_to_string :: #memory(noescape(bytes), reads(bytes), returns_fresh)
        \\c_free :: #memory(kills(p), noescape(p), unknown(arg: p), unknown(reason: "inferred unknown behavior"))
        \\c_malloc :: #memory(released_by(returned.Some, c_free), returns_fresh(returned.Some), unknown(arg: n), unknown(reason: "inferred unknown behavior"))
    );
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\Those are eight consecutive lines of an axiom listing. Both listings
        \\cover the whole program including the standard library, so their exact
        \\length changes with the imported graph; the names are sorted,
        \\which makes the output diffable between builds. `--memory-report` is
        \\the summary form, with the columns `MODULE`, `DECLS`, `CONTRACTED`,
        \\`AXIOM_PRECISE`, `AXIOM_UNKNOWN`, `AXIOM_GRANULAR`, `MISSING`,
        \\`CALL_ASSERTIONS`, `TRUSTED`, `FIRST_RUNG`, `BACKSTOP_HITS`,
        \\`DECLINED_BODIES`, and `VERIFIED_MAGICS`.
    );
}

render_section_127 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#enable(...)` and `#disable(...)` open and close a feature gate.
        \\Four of the gates relax a rule the language otherwise enforces and
        \\one, `memory_strict`, adds rules instead. The set is closed: the
        \\parser recognises exactly five names, and anything else is
        \\`error: unknown feature 'nonsense'`.
    );
    table_header(1, "Gate   —   What `#enable` permits");
    table_row(2, "`lossy_numerics`   |   Implicit integer-to-integer conversions that lose information: narrowing, and sign reinterpretation.");
    table_row(3, "`pointer_punning`   |   Pointee-unchecked pointer conversions, and `*T` interchangeable with `[*]T`.");
    table_row(4, "`memory_strict`   |   Nothing. It requires a `#memory` contract where one is otherwise optional, and forbids overlaying an imported function.");
    table_row(5, "`allow_union_unwrap`   |   Direct tagged-union payload access outside a pattern match.");
    table_row(6, "`private_section`, `private_sections`   |   Declaration visibility. Accepts a second argument, `siblings`. Not a safety gate.");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\The two spellings `private_section` and `private_sections` are
        \\synonyms, and the second argument is legal only on them: any other
        \\pairing is `error: unknown feature 'lossy_numerics,siblings'`.
        \\Visibility is covered in the chapter on visibility and private
        \\sections; the four remaining gates are the subject here, three of
        \\them relaxations of static semantics and one a tightening of the
        \\memory analysis.
    );
    subheading(8, "Form and placement");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\A feature directive is a top-level section directive, not a
        \\statement. It takes effect at the point it appears and stays in
        \\effect until the matching `#disable` or the end of the file. It
        \\cannot appear inside a body.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Name: s64; Code: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\    t := Token.Code(3);
        \\    print("%\n", t.Code);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\error: unexpected directive 'enable'
        \\  --> ...\f1.glo:6:5
        \\  |
        \\6 |     #enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\  |     ^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\The reason is that a gate is a property of a declaration, not of a
        \\region of statements. The parser stamps the currently-open set onto
        \\every declaration it parses, and the checker reads the stamp back
        \\when it checks that declaration. A gate opened inside a body would
        \\have nothing to attach to, and would have to mean something
        \\different from a gate opened outside one.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Token :: union { Name: s64; Code: s64; }
        \\
        \\#enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\inside :: (t: Token) -> s64 { return t.Code; }
        \\
        \\#disable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\after :: (t: Token) -> s64 { return t.Code; }
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\error: tagged-union payload '.Code' may only be bound by a pattern match
        \\   --> ...\f2.glo:11:39
        \\   |
        \\11 | after :: (t: Token) -> s64 { return t.Code; }
        \\   |                                       ^^^^
        \\note: match on the union and use that arm's payload capture, or enable #enable(allow_union_unwrap) for unsafe access
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\`inside` compiles and `after` does not, and the split is decided at
        \\the declaration, not at the call. Deleting `after` leaves a program
        \\in which an ungated `main` calls the gated `inside` and prints `3`.
        \\A gate is not contagious in either direction.
    );
    subheading(16, "The reach of a gate");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Every position that carries an expression the checker evaluates is a
        \\declaration in this sense, so a gate reaches further than function
        \\bodies. It is installed for function bodies, global initialisers,
        \\constant initialisers, struct field defaults, and top-level
        \\`#comptime` blocks.
    );
    table_header(18, "Position   —   Gated");
    table_row(19, "Function body   |   yes");
    table_row(20, "Global variable initialiser   |   yes");
    table_row(21, "`::` constant initialiser   |   yes");
    table_row(22, "Struct field default   |   yes");
    table_row(23, "Top-level `#comptime` block   |   yes");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\This matters because the payload rule is now a default error rather
        \\than a relaxation of a permissive default: a gate that could not
        \\reach an initialiser would leave that position with no escape hatch
        \\at all.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Res :: union { Ok: s64; Err: s64; }
        \\
        \\SOURCE :: Res.Ok(7);
        \\
        \\#enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\WIDTH :: SOURCE.Ok;
        \\
        \\gated_global: s64 = SOURCE.Ok;
        \\
        \\#disable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\ungated_global: s64 = SOURCE.Ok;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% %\n", WIDTH, gated_global);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The constant and the gated global are accepted; only
        \\`ungated_global` is rejected, with the payload-access error pointing
        \\at line 15. Removing that line leaves a program that prints `7 7`.
        \\The same holds for a top-level `#comptime` block: gated, it runs and
        \\prints; after `#disable`, an identical block is an error.
    );
    subheading(27, "What each gate actually relaxes");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`lossy_numerics` is narrower than its name suggests. The gate is
        \\consulted at exactly one place in the checker, inside the branch that
        \\compares an integer type against an integer type. Narrowing and sign
        \\reinterpretation become implicit; float conversions do not, in either
        \\direction and at any width.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#enable(lossy_numerics);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    big: s64 = 300;
        \\    small: u8 = big;
        \\    negative: s64 = -1;
        \\    wrapped: u8 = negative;
        \\    print("% %\n", small, wrapped);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\44 255
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\Under the same gate, `narrow: f32 = wide;` with `wide: f64` is
        \\`error: got f64, expected f32`, and the widening direction is
        \\rejected the same way. There are no implicit float conversions in
        \\Glosso, gate or no gate; the chapter on conversions gives the full
        \\rule and `cast` gives the explicit spelling.
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\`pointer_punning` relaxes the pointee check and the distinction
        \\between a single pointer and a many-pointer. The two assignments below
        \\are rejected without it: `error: got *s64, expected [*]s64` and
        \\`error: got *s64, expected *u8`. With it, both are accepted and the
        \\program prints `65 65`.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#enable(pointer_punning);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    value: s64 = 65;
        \\    single: *s64 = *value;
        \\    many: [*]s64 = single;
        \\    bytes: *u8 = single;
        \\    print("% %\n", many[0], bytes.*);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\What it does not relax is const. The gate is tested inside the
        \\branch that has already established the qualifier direction is
        \\legal, so discarding `const` remains an error under the gate:
        \\`writable: *s64 = readonly;` with `readonly: *const s64` reports
        \\`error: got *const s64, expected *s64`.
    );
    subheading(35, "The gate that tightens");
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\`memory_strict` is the one gate that permits nothing. It turns two
        \\silent defaults of the memory analysis into errors for the
        \\declarations it covers: a declaration with no body that touches
        \\memory must carry a complete contract instead of contributing no
        \\facts, and a function the program exports must carry one at all.
        \\An empty `#memory{}` satisfies the second, because the requirement
        \\is that the contract be written, not that it claim anything.
    );
    code_block(37, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#enable(memory_strict);
        \\
        \\opaque_get :: (n: s64) -> *u8 #foreign "c" "opaque_get";
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", opaque_get(8)); }
    );
    code_block(38, "text", #string
        \\error: [memory.contract] memory-touching axiom 'opaque_get' requires a complete #memory contract under memory_strict
        \\  --> ...\f7.glo:5:1
        \\  |
        \\5 | opaque_get :: (n: s64) -> *u8 #foreign "c" "opaque_get";
        \\  | ^^^^^^^^^^
        \\
        \\error: [memory.contract] exported function 'main' requires a #memory contract under memory_strict
        \\  --> ...\f7.glo:7:1
        \\  |
        \\7 | main :: () { print("%\n", opaque_get(8)); }
        \\  | ^^^^
    );
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\The third rule is about where a contract may be written. An overlay
        \\attaches a contract to a declaration made elsewhere, and under the
        \\gate a module may no longer do that to a function it imported: the
        \\contract belongs beside the declaration, where every caller sees the
        \\same one, rather than in whichever module happened to need it.
    );
    table_header(40, "Rule   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(41, "Bodyless memory-touching declaration   |   `memory-touching axiom 'f' requires a complete #memory contract under memory_strict`");
    table_row(42, "Exported function   |   `exported function 'f' requires a #memory contract under memory_strict`");
    table_row(43, "Overlay on an imported function   |   `[memory.D1] strict module cannot overlay imported function 'f'`");
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\All three are errors and `--borrow-check` does not govern them; the
        \\flag chooses the severity of the temporal findings, and these are
        \\contract diagnostics. Like every gate the stamp is per declaration,
        \\so `#disable(memory_strict)` returns the rest of the file to the
        \\ordinary defaults, and a strict module can call and be called by a
        \\lax one.
    );
    subheading(45, "The checks a gate keeps");
    paragraph(46, #string,oneline
        \\`allow_union_unwrap` removes a compile-time rule and replaces it
        \\with a runtime one; it does not remove the check. A gated payload
        \\read compiles to a tag comparison at `-O0` and `-O1`, and reading
        \\the wrong variant aborts with the site named.
    );
    code_block(47, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\Res :: union { Ok: s64; Err: s64; }
        \\
        \\make :: (flag: bool) -> Res {
        \\    if flag return Res.Ok(5);
        \\    return Res.Err(9);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", make(true).Ok);
        \\    print("%\n", make(false).Ok);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(48, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O1 -o f6.exe f6.glo && ./f6.exe
        \\5
        \\...\f6.glo:14:30: union access: expected .Ok, but the value holds one of .Ok | .Err
        \\$ echo $?
        \\134
        \\
        \\$ glosso -O2 -o f6.exe f6.glo && ./f6.exe
        \\5
        \\9
    );
    paragraph(49, #string,oneline
        \\At `-O2` and above the check is not emitted and the wrong payload is
        \\read, as the second half of the transcript shows. The tag check
        \\belongs to the runtime-check family described in the chapter on
        \\runtime safety checks and panics, which is enabled at `-O0` and
        \\`-O1` only. Use `-O2` or `-O3` to omit it; the size modes are also
        \\configured without the check but currently produce no LLVM 23 artifact.
    );
    note(50, #string,oneline
        \\A gate is not a promise that the compiler will stop looking. If the
        \\tag can be folded at compile time, the gated read is still a hard
        \\error: `R :: Res.Err(9);` followed by `R.Ok` gives `error: this holds
        \\.Err; reading .Ok is never valid`, with the note that the tag is
        \\known at compile time, so the gate's runtime check would fail on
        \\every execution.
    );
    paragraph(51, #string,oneline
        \\The two remaining relaxations keep nothing comparable, because there
        \\is nothing left to check: a narrowed integer and a punned pointer
        \\are ordinary values by the time they reach code generation. Their
        \\cost is paid entirely at the declaration that opened the gate, which
        \\is the argument for keeping such a section short and adjacent to the
        \\code that needs it. `memory_strict` is the mirror image: it costs
        \\nothing at run time and is paid entirely in contracts that have to
        \\be written.
    );
}
